window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['busolt/guyer'] = {"busolt-s01-001":["Logic or",[]],"busolt-s01-002":["Doctrine of Reason by",[]],"busolt-s01-003":["Herr Professor Kant.",[]],"busolt-s01-004":["Königsberg the 8th Sept. 1790.",[]],"busolt-s01-005":["G.C.W. Busolt.",[]],"busolt-s01-006":["[Logik Busolt] Table of Contents",[]],"busolt-s01-007":["Prolegomena",[]],"busolt-s01-008":["608",[]],"busolt-s01-009":["The History of Logic",[]],"busolt-s01-010":["613",[]],"busolt-s01-011":["Utility of Logic",[]],"busolt-s01-012":["613",[]],"busolt-s01-013":["Treatise of Logic",[]],"busolt-s01-014":["616-686",[]],"busolt-s01-015":["On the Perfection of Cognition in General",[]],"busolt-s01-016":["620",[]],"busolt-s01-017":["On the Defects of Cognition in General",[]],"busolt-s01-018":["622",[]],"busolt-s01-019":["On the Horizon of Cognition",[]],"busolt-s01-020":["623",[]],"busolt-s01-021":["On the Misuse or Defect of Cognition",[]],"busolt-s01-022":["626",[]],"busolt-s01-023":["Division of the Learned",[]],"busolt-s01-024":["627",[]],"busolt-s01-025":["On Truth",[]],"busolt-s01-026":["627",[]],"busolt-s01-027":["On the Clarity of Learned Cognition",[]],"busolt-s01-028":["633",[]],"busolt-s01-029":["On the Magnitude of Learned Cognition",[]],"busolt-s01-030":["636",[]],"busolt-s01-031":["On the Certainty of Learned Cognition",[]],"busolt-s01-032":["637",[]],"busolt-s01-033":["On the Different Kinds of Taking to Be True",[]],"busolt-s01-034":["637",[]],"busolt-s01-035":["On Hypotheses",[]],"busolt-s01-036":["647",[]],"busolt-s01-037":["On Persuasion",[]],"busolt-s01-038":["647",[]],"busolt-s01-039":["On Mathematical Certainty",[]],"busolt-s01-040":["649",[]],"busolt-s01-041":["On Practical Cognitions",[]],"busolt-s01-042":["652",[]],"busolt-s01-043":["On Concepts",[]],"busolt-s01-044":["653",[]],"busolt-s01-045":["On Definitions",[]],"busolt-s01-046":["656",[]],"busolt-s01-047":["On Philosophical Definition",[]],"busolt-s01-048":["657",[]],"busolt-s01-049":["The Logical Division of a Concept",[]],"busolt-s01-050":["660",[]],"busolt-s01-051":["On Judgments",[]],"busolt-s01-052":["661",[]],"busolt-s01-053":["The Division of Judgments",[]],"busolt-s01-054":["661",[]],"busolt-s01-055":["On Categorical Judgments",[]],"busolt-s01-056":["664",[]],"busolt-s01-057":["On Hypothetical and Disjunctive Judgments",[]],"busolt-s01-058":["666",[]],"busolt-s01-059":["Rules of Syllogisms",[]],"busolt-s01-060":["673",[]],"busolt-s01-061":["The Four Syllogisms according to Their Figures",[]],"busolt-s01-062":["674",[]],"busolt-s01-063":["Rules of Syllogisms. Continuation",[]],"busolt-s01-064":["674",[]],"busolt-s01-065":["*De locis topicis*",[]],"busolt-s01-066":["681",[]],"busolt-s01-067":["Practical Logic",[]],"busolt-s01-068":["682",[]],"busolt-s01-069":["Remark on Meditating",[]],"busolt-s01-070":["685",[]],"busolt-s01-071":["On the Use of Words",[]],"busolt-s01-072":["685",[]],"busolt-s01-073":["Logik Busolt",[]],"busolt-s01-074":["Prolegomena",[]],"busolt-s02-001":["Everything acts according to certain rules, which are either 1. universal or necessary ones, which are used and are needed with every aim without looking particularly to this or that object. Without these no use of the understanding at all is possible; or 2.) particular ones, or such as with particular aims take regard of this or that object. The former rules are unalterable, the others are altered arbitrarily: for everything particular is contingent. Without the particular the use of the understanding is possible only in a certain science. For the understanding is properly the faculty of rules. The senses, on the other hand, are the faculty of intuition. E.g. In mathematics one can, without using the eyes or instruments, comprehend that a triangle has three sides and just as many angles; but if one wants to know how a triangle is measured; then the use of the eyes is necessarily required for it. The student learns mathematics as it can be comprehended through the understanding, the surveyor as it can be through the senses, and so both act according to particular rules. Natural powers one observes before one knows them. Acquired powers one must know before their use. The animals the inanimate nature act according to laws, according to rules, without being conscious of them. To the human being they are also often unknown, although he acts according to them. One acts according to natural rules:",[]],"busolt-s02-002":["1.) Because they are already in nature.",[]],"busolt-s02-003":["2.) From imitation, where the exercise occurs without acquaintance with the rules.",[]],"busolt-s02-004":["3.) Through one's own use and exercise, here we often err, but hereby we attain to skill. Yet faults of habit excepted. Logic is the science of the rules of the use of the understanding and of reason",[]],"busolt-s02-005":["Here below there is found in us the faculty to act, and from this arises natural logic. For logic is the science of rules. The human being acts in the beginning according to rules of which he is not conscious; thus for so long he has a natural logic; if he comes to a riper age he corrects this through rules, and so arises artificial logic. Thus someone speaks German and afterwards teaches himself rules from a German grammar. But grammar is only a discipline, logic a science; doctrine. The difference between discipline and science or doctrine is this; with the former one cannot know why something is so and not otherwise, with the other, however, one has an inner proof of it. with the former we consult experience, with the other the understanding alone, the former is particular the other universal, the first is called *scientia empirica*, the other *scientia scientifica*. The human being has powers of which some serve to create materials, others to order them. The first is done by sensibility, the other by reason and the power of judgment.",[]],"busolt-s02-006":["Artificial logic now (for it is this that is spoken of here) deals with universal rules, consequently with such as must be used in all sciences, and in the judging of all objects. It accordingly separates off everything contingent, everything particular, and thus abstracts also from the difference of objects. It chooses only the form but not the matter or the content of things. In every cognition there is to be distinguished the material, e.g. If bodies are heavy; then they are driven by others, and the formal, e.g. If God is just then he punishes evil actions. Matter is the object of cognition, form, the way in which we cognize objects. E.g. physics cognizes the objects of outer sense, but psychology those of inner sense. Both we cognize empirically, this is the form, which is thus the same in both. Accordingly logic is, in this consideration, the science of the form of the understanding. Its rules are thus *a priori*, i.e. its universal rules accordingly have reason for their *principia*, whereas grammar, and every other empirical science have rules *a posteriori*, i.e. such as have experience for their *principia*. Experience teaches us how a thing actually is, reason, however, how it must be, or whence it thus is. That e.g. water is fluid experience teaches, but whence it is fluid reason teaches. How human beings act morally we see from experience, how they ought to act we see from reason. All our cognitions have two sources the senses and the understanding; both together make up the faculty of cognition. This cognizing is called *cognoscere*. The senses give the matter for thinking but the understanding thinks. The senses give the representation, the latter the rule. The rules of cognition are either",[]],"busolt-s02-007":["1.) Rules of sensibility, and that is called aesthetic fromαἰσθητός, *instrumentum sensorium*, or",[]],"busolt-s02-008":["2.) Rules of the understanding, and that is called logic.This logic contains the rules of all cognitions and is distinguished from aesthetic in this way, that in it everything is taken from reason, and is comprehended, but the latter is grounded on experience and reason. Logic is thus called the doctrine of reason, aesthetic the doctrine of taste.",[]],"busolt-s02-009":["All sciences that expound rules are either 1. canon or a sum total of rules *a priori* which teach us to judge whether something is in conformity with reason or not; or 2. organon or a sum total of rules which serve for the perfecting of this or that science.",[]],"busolt-s02-010":["General logic, is it now a canon or organon? If it is to be an organon, then it would have to give us a direction how e.g. mathematics or metaphysics can be brought to greater perfection. But this it does not do, rather its object is merely the form of the understanding, which must be the same with each and every object Its object is thus universal, consequently it is also no science; for every science has a particular object. Every science thus also extends our cognitions; but logic only corrects them. General logic is thus also not organon, not *norma*, for every norm is grounded on experience. There it can also be called norm *a priori*. An organon also takes place only when a science has already been invented, and brought to a certain height; so that it now perfects itself, as e.g. Bacon of Verulam wrote an *Organon scientiarum*, where among other things he gives directions how physics could be brought to a high degree. Logic is rather a canon, i.e. it contains rules that are grounded on the understanding alone, and also can be demonstrated only *a priori*. The examples in logic are taken from the universal use of the understanding in common life. It is thus propaedeutic of all sciences. Aesthetic is not organon. For otherwise it follows from this that one could learn to be witty, also not canon; for it does not expound rules *a priori*; rather it is a critique or knowledge of rules, and indeed of the most universal ones, to form taste. It accordingly puts us in a position to judge a product of utility",[]],"busolt-s02-011":["properly. Remark: canon is *doctrina demonstrabilis* and serves for *judication*, organon for the *construction* of cognitions, consequently they invent nothing, logic is accordingly not *ars heuristica*, or art of inventing. It was thus useful even to the power of invention excellently for it orders it and alters; what has been invented it separates off. Aesthetic is the doctrine of the sensible power of judgment, whether a work of genius, or something else pleases, or not. It is thus the sensible power of cognition as taste is the sensible power of judging. With taste experiment and experience are always needed: for even if at times a piece is composed according to all the rules of music, still it does not please; rather *Plurima vota valent*. Aesthetic can also contain only empirical rules; i.e. rules *a posteriori*, and one could in such a way say it is critique of the beautiful: for although it depends here on experience, the critic can still adduce grounds whence this or that pleases or displeases. Nevertheless there is something which a poet says: *malo convivis quam coquis placere, fercula mea*.",[]],"busolt-s02-012":["In logic one must mix in no foreign science, thus e.g. empirical psychology does not belong in logic: for this is the doctrine of the phenomena of the human soul, or it expounds universal rules of the actual procedure of the human soul. Above all logic teaches not how we actually proceed, but how we ought to proceed This even the word rule already indicates: For a rule is the condition under which something ought to be brought forth. We divide the rules of the understanding into subjective, of the actual procedure of the understanding. Into objective, of the correct rules according to which one ought to proceed. Logic is a doctrine of the universal laws of the understanding in general. It looks only to the form of the understanding with an object, not to the content of it, e.g. whether an inference was made according to laws of inference. The logical use of the understanding is either, universal, and that is called *sensus communis*, *sana ratio*, *bon sens*, i.e. one can thereby comprehend something straightaway, and this common understanding is the so-called middle understanding. That is, if each had equally much understanding, then there would be just as much understanding among human beings as there is now, since it is distributed so unequally by nature yet each has enough of it, and no one complains of lack of understanding for only with his understanding can he measure the boundaries of the understanding. To this sound understanding one refers in more recent times, as it were as a divine revelation, and one believes oneself entitled not to exercise oneself further in reflection, and to take pains, because after all everyone could easily see it. Or 2. speculatively the difference between the common and the speculative understanding; it comes down to this, that the speculative cognizes things *in abstracto*, the common *in concreto*, e.g. the rule from natural right: *Casum sentit dominus* the speculative understanding understands without application to this or that particular case of common life. But the common understanding must first have examples and proofs. Remark One can refer to the sound understanding only then, when something can be cognized *in concreto*. The speculative understanding must seek to accommodate itself to the common, i.e. it must make comprehensible *in concreto* what it has cognized *in abstracto*, and this is called popularity. Precisely this is a proof that our speculative cognition is clear and distinct. According to this twofold use of the understanding logic is divided into the natural and the speculative. Logic proper is the doctrine of the speculative use of the understanding: for the natural each already has himself it is also speculative for this reason, because it expounds rules *in abstracto*.",[]],"busolt-s02-013":["Among the Aristotelians logic was distinguished into analytic and dialectic, i.e. according to the author's definition, into the logic 1. of certain and 2. of probable cognition. But in this way one cannot distinguish them perfectly enough: for both in such a way have truth for object and it differs only in regard to certainty, in that one presents the one through insufficient, the other through sufficient grounds. The proper definition of analytic and dialectic is to be this: Analytic is the logic of truth Dialectic is the logic of illusion what now is logic according to this definition is it analytic or dialectic? Considered as canon it is an analytic, and as organon a dialectic. If one regards logic, considered as organon, as the logic of truth, then an advocate becomes a pettifogger a philosopher a sophist for in such a way one judges of objects according to universal rules, without knowing the object itself, as e.g. recently disputations etc. etc. everything was expounded according to mathematical order. Accordingly the form was preserved. But the matter falsely expounded Nevertheless exercise in the logic of illusion is not to be censured. Logic is called doctrine of reason, but not in regard to the order of its exposition: for every science must be expounded in conformity with reason, because it has reason for its object. Instead of doctrine of reason one could also call it doctrine of the understanding: for the understanding we use with all objects, but not reason. One may not abstract entirely in logic, but one can also make excursions into psychology and into the rules *in concreto*: inasmuch as logic is properly the doctrine of the border-neighborhood of reason with sensibility, psychology and history. Logic is the canon of reason.",[]],"busolt-s03-001":["Aristotle was the first logician, he is the father of logic: His logic is a product of great acuity. The scholastics adopted his theorems, and sought to elevate logic above all but they gained nothing thereby. Bacon of Verulam also took some pains in logic. Descartes in his book *de methodo* did the same Malebranche writes in his work: *Recherches de la vérité* more metaphysics than logic. Von Tschirnhaus writes *Medicinam mentis et corporis mathematice illustratur*, which is much to be recommended. Locke and Leibniz have also caused very much in logic. Wolff's logic is the best most of these men did not set the proper limitations for logic. It is so to speak the anatomy of the understanding Crusius also pleases but only his adherents Lambert's Organon contains a multitude of insights.",[]],"busolt-s04-001":["This is clear: for the form of the understanding is more important than the content, whereas a logical error is the very worst: for one not only makes it; but one also makes it a rule for oneself to sin. It also has a universal utility, if it is expounded popularly. All cognition is accordingly divided",[]],"busolt-s04-002":["1.) Into *cognitionem ex datis*, i.e. *cognitio a posteriori*.",[]],"busolt-s04-003":["2.) Into *cognitionem ex principiis*, i.e. *cognitio a priori* or cognitions of reason *antequam nobis data sunt*.",[]],"busolt-s04-004":["*Cognitio ex datis* is when an object is given to us, e.g. which we have from the narration of others, e.g. the motion of the stars, but whoever knows how to state why an eclipse of the sun arose precisely at that time the proves *ex principiis*. *cognitio a posteriori* is the empirical, *cognitio a priori* is the speculative cognition of reason. This we have either 1. from concepts, i.e. philosophical cognition",[]],"busolt-s04-005":["2. From construction, that is mathematical cognition. Mathematics and philosophy thus differ greatly: for philosophy is cognized from concepts, mathematics from the construction of concepts. But both are not distinguished according to the object. For philosophy too has magnitudes for its object. Both are cognitions of reason, and indeed both speculative, the difference lies in this, that the one rests on concepts, the other on construction of concepts. E.g. If I wanted to ponder about a triangle how large indeed the angle would be that contained all three in itself, then philosophically I would find out nothing in order to have a correct concept of it. I must thus construct the concepts, i.e. I must see the things *in singulari coram intuitu*. *Intuitus a priori* is when I think and paint the figure to myself not from experience. *Intuitus empiricus*, however, when I make the figure from experience. Mathematics has to do with the *intuitus a priori*. Otherwise one is accustomed to distinguish philosophy and mathematics according to the object, by saying philosophy treats of qualities, mathematics of quantities. But this difference is incorrect, for both philosophy and mathematics treat of magnitudes, but mathematics especially, because I can construct a concept only according to magnitude. Philosophy is a science, and must make up a system. It is thus the system of all speculative cognition from concepts. Our thinking is properly a cognition through concepts, or a discursive cognition. But the senses give us only cognition through intuition, or intuitive cognitions. For him who possesses the former one could call a philosopher, but him who has the other, a man of sound understanding. Philosophy is not practical, rather it is the legal science of reason. The laws according to which we proceed are",[]],"busolt-s04-006":["1.) Rules of skill, i.e. political laws.",[]],"busolt-s04-007":["2.) Rules of wisdom, i.e. moral laws.",[]],"busolt-s04-008":["In philosophy there is to be distinguished objective and subjective cognition Objectively this cognition can be a cognition of reason",[]],"busolt-s04-009":["*a priori*, subjectively philosophy can be nothing but a historical cognition. Philosophy thus cognizes *ex conceptu*, mathematics *ex intuitu*. In philosophy an *intuitus* can be only an example, whereas in mathematics an *intuitus* is the essential thing, hence Wolff errs when in his Extract of all Mathematical Sciences he gives of similarity only a thoroughly speculative definition. Euclid does it quite otherwise, who goes at once to the application. Philosophical cognition is accordingly discursive, mathematical intuitive. Philosophy cannot be learned by heart, and a philosopher cannot be an adherent of a particular sect; for so he will learn philosophy, but not to philosophize, in mathematics on the other hand one can follow the utility, the necessity of another's theorem, since in mathematics as an intuitive science one can convince oneself through the guiding thread of intuition whether this or that one has judged correctly; whereas in philosophy as a discursive science this guiding thread is lacking. If one finds *qui dicunt academia*, it signifies the Platonists, *qui dicunt Porticus* signifies Stoics. If one finds *qui dicunt horti* it signifies the Epicureans.",[]],"busolt-s04-010":["Philosophy is a speculative cognition of reason according to rules *in abstracto*. It must be a system, that is: everything in it must be expounded according to a lawful order, for through the aesthetic the system is distinguished from the aggregate, in which everything is thrown together, which is also called rhapsody.",[]],"busolt-s04-011":["Philosophy is a system of cognitions of reason, yet objectively it can be a cognition of reason and not subjectively: for even philosophy, which is a cognition of reason in the strictest sense, can be learned by hearsay from another, so in regard to this it is subjective, accordingly no cognition of reason. A philosopher is either a practical one, i.e. one who through concepts is master over his passions, or a theoretical one, who is the lawgiver or rather the one learned in the laws of human reason. The natural scientist is no philosopher, for he uses reason as much as he can with natural objects, whereas the metaphysician lets his reason as it were consider itself, and hence bears the name philosopher by right. He is a *legislator* of humanity, a sophist on the other hand a *leguleius* of it.",[]],"busolt-s05-001":["Here cognition in general is spoken of. Logic itself treats of representations, but not of such of which we are not conscious, i.e. of obscure ones, but of clear ones, i.e. of such of which we have a consciousness. Every cognition has an object and can therefore be considered in a twofold way: namely",[]],"busolt-s05-002":["1.) In relation to the object, that is the material.",[]],"busolt-s05-003":["2.) In relation to the form of the understanding, i.e. the formal of cognition.",[]],"busolt-s05-004":["In logic one abstracts from the object, and looks to the form of the understanding, i.e. *modus cognoscendi*. This formal of cognition is now again:",[]],"busolt-s05-005":["1. *Logicum* thinking intellectual",[]],"busolt-s05-006":["2. *Aestheticum* intuiting sensual",[]],"busolt-s05-007":["Representations are:",[]],"busolt-s05-008":["1. Subjective, i.e. sensation, it is the alteration of the state in the subject.",[]],"busolt-s05-009":["2. Objective, i.e. cognition, it reflects the representation onto the object.",[]],"busolt-s05-010":["Sensation one can communicate only to him who has or has had it, consequently nothing regular can be established about it either in logic or in aesthetic, because one subject always remains distinguished from others, and thus also the sensation. Every representation is always cognition as intuition: in such a way the representations are",[]],"busolt-s05-011":["1.) of intuition (sensibility)",[]],"busolt-s05-012":["2.) of concepts (understanding)",[]],"busolt-s05-013":["1.) Intuition goes to a single object, insofar as it is distinguished from others,",[]],"busolt-s05-014":["2.) Concepts go to something universal, for they make the particular universal. From such divisions there then arise;",[]],"busolt-s05-015":["a) Aesthetic, i.e. that discipline which expounds the rule of perfection from the representations of intuiting.",[]],"busolt-s05-016":["b) Logic is that science which expounds the rules of perfection from the representations of the understanding.",[]],"busolt-s05-017":["Logic accordingly has to do with clear representations, i.e. with such concepts of which we are conscious, and these we divide again into the distinct, and indistinct.",[]],"busolt-s05-018":["A distinct concept is that according to which we cognize the manifold that a thing contains, e.g. with the concept of virtue I think three things",[]],"busolt-s05-019":["1.) Regularity.",[]],"busolt-s05-020":["2.) The freedom to act.",[]],"busolt-s05-021":["3.) The mastery of desires and passions.",[]],"busolt-s05-022":["If one has merely a concept of the whole but not of the parts, then the concept is called a clear one indeed, but indistinct, one does not properly call it confused; for confusion is opposed to order but not to distinctness. All cognitions one attains through order, i.e. *per reductionem idearum subactis quosdam titulos*. Between distinct and confused there is yet a middle thing, namely simple. A simple representation is that in which no manifoldness is contained. E.g. Of a single book I cannot say that it lies in disorder. Distinctness of intuition does not take place without concepts of the parts. – – Distinctness of concepts is not possible otherwise than that one at the same time cognize and distinguish the manifold. Philosophy is concerned with holding our understanding to it, in order to resolve our universal concepts and to show us the whole store of our cognition. *Est enim vt aiebat Socrates; Philosophia cognitionem nostrarum, quasi quaedam obstetrix non mater.*",[]],"busolt-s05-023":["Thus we learn in philosophy the difference between pride, haughtiness, arrogance, although we had already previously obtained some concepts of these. But in philosophy from obscure concepts clear ones are made, from clear ones distinct ones, and in it a consciousness of our cognition is as it were imparted to us. The difference of logical and aesthetic representations consists in this,",[]],"busolt-s05-024":["1.) The aesthetic representation is the representation through the senses or imagination, this *imagination* belongs as it were also to the senses, it is their copyist, it is the source of intuition.",[]],"busolt-s05-025":["2.) The logical representation is the representation through the understanding, and thinking is therefore called cognizing through concepts.",[]],"busolt-s05-026":["The first kind of representation is also called the sensible faculty of cognition, or the lower power of the soul, whereas the second kind the upper power of the soul.",[]],"busolt-s05-027":["Reason is in relation to the understanding that which the understanding is for the senses; both kinds are needed for a concept, and otherwise no concept can be brought forth. The aesthetic perfections are either essential, i.e. the *conformity* of the sensible with the logical, or intellectual, a piece cannot please with the wittiest ideas if one cannot find the agreement of the whole imagining with the theme. Precisely from this essential perfection arises the *sensus publicus*, universal taste: for this is the faculty of choice according to the universal laws of sensibility, or the aesthetic perfections are also contingent These one cannot determine, for they go to the subject.",[]],"busolt-s05-028":["The universal and particular laws of aesthetic.",[]],"busolt-s05-029":["The universal laws are possible in regard to intuition. For in the judging of the beautiful, which is investigated in aesthetic (whereas in logic the true), intuition is the *principal*, sensation *accessorium*. Intuition cannot be divided; but sensations cannot be imparted, if it happens, it happens through similes. The private laws rest on the different sensations of each. Sensations are certain alterations of our state, through which all our powers of cognition are enlivened and exerted. Taste is the faculty of the sensible power of judging according to universal laws of sensibility. Feeling is the faculty of sensation according to private laws of sensibility.",[]],"busolt-s05-030":["From this arises the mixture of our cognitions out of sensations and concepts. If our cognition is separated from all sensations, then it is pure. The purer the cognition, the less it is sensation. The stronger the sensation is; the less and correctly pure is the cognition. Accordingly we must not let ourselves be carried away by the dazzling magic power of the senses, but first ground the cognition itself, and then build the sensation upon it, and so arises the proper edification. Sensations surprise, cognition comes little by little; the former are something transitory, the latter something constant. The general aesthetic perfection consists in charm and emotion. (*Delinementum est mentis concussio.*) *Attrahit delinementum, nos quoad contemplandum longius objectum detinent. Convolvit concussio animum nobisque quasi quendam horrorem infundit. Mens delinementa sentit, quandam animae voluptatem, atque in objectum quasi acquiescit. Concussio est tamquam animae aliqua actio. Delinemento, ne ob dulce animis condimentum fastidium moveat, concussionis atque commutationis aliquid admisceatur, conquassatio haec est, habitur quidam dolor, puncto temporis inquinatam voluptatem resolvitur.*",[]],"busolt-s05-031":["On the manifold kinds of cognitions. A rational and a cognition of reason are very different. A rational cognition is when it is only in conformity with reason, i.e. that which the human being can see through his reason, and cognitions of reason are those which are derived from reason; in every science one requires such. The author says a learned cognition and a philosophical cognition were one and the same, but they are very different. For all our cognition is either historical or cognition of reason, the latter must be speculative from concepts if it is to be philosophy. To historical cognition belongs literature, this comes from *litterator*, i.e. one who knows books, this one is a philologist if he is a critic, and knows especially the ancient languages. It is already a great degree of such a learned one up to the polyhistor, this one is as it were a giant of learning. He does not learn philosophy and mathematics, rather he knows them already historically. He has a knowledge of books and himself judges the ancient philosophers. They are nothing but half-learned, they know historically what great men have said. But the ground is lacking to them. By the way of imitation I can become no philosopher, I must owe nothing to others, but through them only improve their concepts. A philosopher is no learned man, for in philosophy one does not need to be instructed. A learned man is one who has been taught. They know nothing other than what has been taught them. But philosophy and mathematics I can have from myself, only history can I learn. A learned man is *eruditus* not *litteratus*, the latter is he who can read and write. Mathematics is no learning, but a cognition of reason, philosophy is also a cognition of reason; but in philosophy one can speak discursively, in mathematics intuitively. A mathematical babbler one cannot think, but indeed a philosophical one, namely if one learns the expressions that they have all used and adds something new. They make a show of sciences. The beautiful sciences are no learning. It is good if a poet, an orator, is a learned man, but what is beautiful, what moves, he must have from reason. They are arts and not sciences. With them it depends not on knowing something, but on being able to do something. Wit, taste, genius belong to it. Aesthetic is a critique of art. Logical perfection makes the particular universal, goes from intuition to concepts. Aesthetic perfection on the other hand the reverse. The greatest perfection is the combination of logical perfection with the aesthetic, in that only then does one comprehend and become intelligible. All cognition that concerns our understanding refers to the object, all cognition that concerns our senses refers to the subject, logic aims at cognizing the particular through universal concepts. Aesthetic cognizes the universal through the particular, it only fills the soul with intuitions. Logic seeks to bring all cognition to concepts, aesthetic all to intuitions. Both must be able to be united if I want to cognize the universal *in concreto*. Only he can make cognition sensible who makes it intuitive. A cognition that is indeed logically perfect but not aesthetically, one calls well thorough, but dry. On the other side a cognition is beautiful, stimulating, agreeable, engaging, but it lacks logical perfection; then it is shallow. The entertaining does not indeed make up the essential; but our cognition still loses if it is lacking. To beauty is opposed lack of it and on the other side ugliness. The first one calls dryness. Affectation is an ugliness. One can narrate the most gruesome representations, and yet clothe them beautifully; only not disgust, this can be concealed by no art.",[]],"busolt-s06-001":["Cognition one calls the whole domain of the concept which someone reaches of a thing and which comprehends in itself all propositions about it that have become known to him; the greater the number of these propositions, the more exhaustive and complete, the more sharply one can prove them, the more certain, and the more frequent application to occurring cases these propositions admit, the more useful is our cognition. The perfection of cognition specifies itself into quality and quantity. The perfection of cognition in regard to quality is the same in all cognitions; in regard to quantity it depends with each object on the multitude and the degree. In regard to quality the perfection of cognition is theoretical and practical. The first refers to the understanding, the other to the will. The first is weighed only in logic, the other determines actions.",[]],"busolt-s06-002":["Theoretical cognition is the fruit of speculation or of reflection; the practical grows out of the applications which one finds occasion to make in exercise. The former is obtained by laying certain concepts at the ground, drawing consequences from them, and then setting inferences upon inferences; the latter consists in a collection of experiences, and consequences drawn from them. Theoretical perfection is either aesthetic or logical",[]],"busolt-s06-003":["1.) Logical perfection contains",[]],"busolt-s06-004":["a.) The matter (*obiectum*), which consists in truth, and thus in regard to quality.",[]],"busolt-s06-005":["b.) The form (*modum cognoscendi*), which consists:",[]],"busolt-s06-006":["aa.) In universality, that we cognize everything from concepts.",[]],"busolt-s06-007":["bb.) In distinctness, that we make these abstract concepts representable *in concreto*",[]],"busolt-s06-008":["2.) Aesthetic perfection contains",[]],"busolt-s06-009":["a.) The material, this is here sensation,",[]],"busolt-s06-010":["b.) The formal, that is",[]],"busolt-s06-011":["aa.) Manifoldness, of this logical universality is the opposite. It must in its *definitionen* send away everything else, abbreviate everything, whereas the former extends [*etendirt*] and enlarges everything, through synonyms etc. etc.",[]],"busolt-s06-012":["bb.) Unity: for all these enlargements must after all all make up a whole e.g. The description of spring from the mouth of the poet and of the astronomer.",[]],"busolt-s06-013":["The essential [element] of concepts is universality; the essential [*Essentielle*] [element] of intuition manifoldness aesthetic perfection as to form, pairs best with logic. Logical perfection combined with the aesthetic is called cogency. aesthetic and logic combined is called: manifoldness. Manifoldness and universality are so combined that neither does the other harm",[]],"busolt-s06-014":["[does]. Distinctness in logic is that which in aesthetic is liveliness. The livelier the representation is the more emotion it excites, but the less I cognize. It is thus the enlivening of the senses. Distinctness however occupies us with the object",[]],"busolt-s06-015":["Aesthetic perfection requires,",[]],"busolt-s06-016":["1.) Taste and this is the form,",[]],"busolt-s06-017":["2.) Feeling and that is the matter: for there belong to it charm and emotion.",[]],"busolt-s06-018":["A practical perfection of logic is not possible, for it would be the movement of willing, this is expounded in morals. The art of the beautiful is sometimes appropriate. To use the painterly beauty of nature in poetry is not well appropriate. For no art attains through depicting and describing the beauties of nature. We seem as it were to want to compete with nature, whether we too can make such an intuiting concept, which would follow nature, for which reason Haller is criticized in his Swiss poems. In regard to human actions art can not only attain the same; but also surpass it; e.g. In the former we cannot be so beheld with our depicting as in intuition, e.g. Milton has magnificently described the sphere and the fall of the same. As far as the highest being we may not venture with painterly beauties; hence an astronomer would be able to depict God better than a poet, for the first says: Behold so great are the works of the Lord; whereas the other with his sensible images would depict too lowly, since after all sensibility cannot be thought in the concept of God.",[]],"busolt-s07-001":["Lacks (*defectus*) must be well distinguished from faults (*vitiis*). With a lack the cognition is still correct, it is only missing for me. With a fault however it is erroneous. E.g. in geography I can leave out important cities, then this is a lack, but if I place a place wrongly, then this is a fault. The lack can be made up, but the fault must first be reduced to a lack. An error is a hindrance to true cognition; before one confirms oneself in error, then, one would rather remain in a degree of ignorance.",[]],"busolt-s08-001":["A horizon is in general a circle which bounds all objects that we can see. Here the horizon is: *Congruentia cognitionis cum terminis perfectionis humanae*. This horizon divides into the logical according to the faculty of cognition, into the practical according to utility. The logical horizon must precede the determination of the practical: for utility only follows upon cognition. I must here not first ask about utility. Utilities often remain hidden for a very long time. When Archimedes found the relation of the cone to the cylinder, it would have been hard for him to state the utility of it; long afterward the same showed itself in common life. The practical part does not belong to logic, nothing more will therefore be said of it. But as concerns the logical, we divide it:",[]],"busolt-s08-002":["1.) Into the universal horizon of the human understanding in general. This can be determined only through deep metaphysical investigations.",[]],"busolt-s08-003":["2.) Into the practical horizon of this or that aim. Here the difference is to be noted:",[]],"busolt-s08-004":["a.) How I determine the horizon of my",[]],"busolt-s08-005":["b.) How I determine the horizon of others' knowledge [*Kenntniße*]. I can determine the horizon",[]],"busolt-s08-006":["1.) From the aims of life,",[]],"busolt-s08-007":["2.) From sex.",[]],"busolt-s08-008":["3.) From station.",[]],"busolt-s08-009":["4.) From the age of a human being.",[]],"busolt-s08-010":["Rules in the determination of the horizon. One should not determine the horizon too wide from the beginning E.g. In the schools one learns many things which one afterwards lets lie, as e.g. the oriental languages. One should therefore always determine the horizon narrow. One should also not determine one's horizon all too narrow, because",[]],"busolt-s08-011":["a.) Much of the knowledge [*Kenntnißen*] gets lost",[]],"busolt-s08-012":["b.) Because we do not know for what we can still use these cognitions.",[]],"busolt-s08-013":["One should not change the horizon of one's knowledge [*Kenntnisse*] too often. E.g. when out of a theologian a jurist comes, and sometimes the reverse. The universal logical horizon we can also determine:",[]],"busolt-s08-014":["1.) Logically or *a priori* and this is the horizon of reason.",[]],"busolt-s08-015":["2.) Empirically or *a posteriori*.",[]],"busolt-s08-016":["aa.) With individual objects.",[]],"busolt-s08-017":["bb.) In regard to the whole human race, namely how far from the beginning up to our times the sciences have risen.",[]],"busolt-s08-018":["The first namely the horizon of reason is determined particularly in metaphysics: for such one does not ask how far the human understanding has brought it, but how far it could bring it. The horizon of reason is:",[]],"busolt-s08-019":["a.) The horizon of healthy reason and",[]],"busolt-s08-020":["b.) The horizon of speculative reason.",[]],"busolt-s08-021":["The horizon of healthy reason is more narrowly bounded than that of the speculative. Everything rises: natural doctrine, natural history, mathematics, history and one will necessarily have to pour everything into narrower form, so that the sciences do not fall into decay, merely because they have risen so high. For human beings would necessarily have to be discouraged, if they might not make the name of a discoverer brilliant. One thus needs in present times much philosophy, in order to choose out of so many sciences the most necessary and most useful. To cognize ignorance, one must know much, for an ignorant person does not cognize his ignorance. He cannot comprehend how he should lack so much in knowledge of the world. The common man e.g. takes the stars for points and believes that another also takes them for such. The more we cognize; the more we see that we are still ignorant, that of the great field of possible and useful sciences we still cultivate only a small part. In order to know [*kennen*] one's ignorance, one must know [*kennen*] at least an outline of the sciences, so that we cognize what we know and what we lack, we must at least know [*kennen*] the sciences extensively. Ignorance in and for itself is something blameworthy, but not every ignorance is to be regretted for he who always digs behind the actions of human beings and seeks out the source of the same will in the end, if he finds everywhere only self-interestedly one's own best and one's own aims as motives for actions, take virtue for a chimera, and the exercise of the same for folly. Perfect virtue no one has ever had and it also cannot be exercised. Scholars who know their ignorance not seldom become confused, e.g. Condamine the famous French naturalist who upon beholding the inexhaustible store of natural things in Peru, lost all desire for the extension of natural history. They will set us ever more *Problemata* which we cannot solve. Of the extensiveness of knowledge",[]],"busolt-s08-022":["1.) Erudition is the sum total of all historical sciences.",[]],"busolt-s08-023":["2.) The philology of the ancient languages is the sum total of the instruments of erudition.",[]],"busolt-s08-024":["A philologist is,",[]],"busolt-s08-025":["a.) A *Litterator*, i.e. one who has book-knowledge of the ancient writings,",[]],"busolt-s08-026":["b.) A linguist when he knows the ancient languages and indeed critically,",[]],"busolt-s08-027":["c.) A humanist, when he studies the beauty of the ancient writings, and then",[]],"busolt-s08-028":["d.) A belletrist who forms his taste from the more recent writings.",[]],"busolt-s08-029":["The whole of polyhistory seems to be an all too great cognition. It is however only a cognition of all sciences. One could call it a Cyclops because it lacks an eye, namely critical philosophy. Philosophy is also a polyhistory, but only historical philosophy. Beside polyhistory is set polymathy. The former is a cognition of all historical sciences, the latter a cognition of all sciences of reason, or of all opinions of the philosophers. A scholar who is both together is a pansophist.",[]],"busolt-s08-030":["For this however more memory than genius is required. Encyclopedic skill is an aptitude for bringing the sciences into small compass. It is rhapsodistic and a mere compilation, but it ought to be a system; and must therefore not be rhapsodistic, but methodical. The encyclopedia brings all sciences under one title, it gives the family tree of them. Such a tree is organic. The dead languages are this vehicle of polyhistory and the commerce of the beautiful sciences, because they",[]],"busolt-s08-031":["1.) Are the instrument of the sciences and",[]],"busolt-s08-032":["2.) Do not change.",[]],"busolt-s09-001":["To this belong pedantry and charlatanry. The word pedant comes from the Italian. *Magister pedarius* was one who led children and was merely in their company who consequently took on manners which do not fit with public ones. For commonly they are accustomed to observe an all too anxious meticulousness in formulas, to which a purposive punctiliousness is opposed Charlatanry borders on pedantry. For whoever possesses the former has indeed no scholastically correct knowledge, but nevertheless seeks through the illusion of the possession of the same to deceive others. Pedantry however presupposes scholastically correct knowledge. They approach especially to *popularitaet* in that it presents knowledge which not seldom requires deep reflection playfully and as easily comprehensible to the grasp of everyone. This is a chief character of the French. The author says: Pedantry is a charlatanry. But both are very different. The former is a learned but limited cognition without *popularitaet*. The latter is a boasting in cognition and consists in this, that one seeks to bring one's own limited skill in everywhere. The perfection of cognition must have a certain art of relation. It is either:",[]],"busolt-s09-002":["a.) Scholastic, when it makes up a part of erudition, or, *coascendentia ad certum conditionis gradum* or,",[]],"busolt-s09-003":["b.) Popular, i.e. the condescension to universal concepts.",[]],"busolt-s09-004":["This condescension is a skill of accommodating oneself,",[]],"busolt-s09-005":["aa.) Both to the sum total, and also",[]],"busolt-s09-006":["bb.) To the capacity and comprehensibility of the *publici* and of the multitude.",[]],"busolt-s09-007":["Scholastic knowledge can be great as to its degree, small as to application. If one wants to be popular, then one must subtract something from thoroughness, exhaustiveness, determinateness. Scholastic cognition has thoroughness as its essential [element]; *popularitaet* adorns and decorates, only thoroughness *Soliditaet* is led with the go-cart of terminology. A sermon must not only be scholastically correct, but also popular, and there belongs to it a sustained condescension. A preacher cannot be a gallant orator. For the more dignity a matter itself has, the less ornament it needs. In general *Soliditaet* is always to be preferred to *popularitaet*. For the more popular the less solid. The skill of accommodating oneself to all the world is a knowledge of the world, and one who possesses this one calls a scholar of the world. Pedantry is thus the lack of the *Popularitaet* of learned cognition.",[]],"busolt-s10-001":["1.) Into guild scholars, who seek their bread through erudition.",[]],"busolt-s10-002":["2.) Into guild-free scholars, who use erudition merely for pleasure.",[]],"busolt-s10-003":["The former must necessarily be of more use to the world than these: for they see themselves compelled to reflect more deeply on the matter, in that they are obliged to show their listeners the object as it were in various positions. The others are admittedly prouder: for they say the free arts must not be used with the aim, as crafts.",[]],"busolt-s11-001":["Truth is the agreement of cognition with the object [*Obiect*]. To truth illusion is contradictorily opposed. On the question of universal criteria the philosopher founders. They have been able to find no universal criterion of truth. A universal criterion is where a mark is provided, without consideration of the object [*Gegenstand*] itself; it must be indifferent toward all objects [*Gegenstände*], the definition must here be abstracted from all differences of determinate objects [*Gegenstände*]; it occupies itself therefore merely with form. The universal criterion of truth thus already lies in the question. He wants to have signs of truth, and this is the agreement of cognition with the object [*Obiect*]. He wants to have universal characteristics i.e. he wants to have a sign which goes universally to all objects [*Gegenstände*], he wants to have a sign of form. The universal criterion of truth is here thus merely formal, the agreement of cognition with the object [*obiect*]. Logic can thus give merely formal not material universal *Kriteria* of truth. It gives merely a relative criterion, universal material criteria of truth logic cannot give. For a cognition can be false relative to one object [*obiect*] but true to another. Every object [*Obiect*] has its material criterion. The material criterion cannot hold of all objects [*obiecten*]. Material truth consists merely in the distinction of one object [*Gegenstand*] from others. Whoever therefore demands of the logician a universal criterion of truth, must not demand a cognition of the object [*Obiect*], whether it is correct, also no universal criterion of truth. The universal criterion of truth in logic is an agreement of cognition with itself. This mark gives *indirecte* a distinction, that if a cognition is contrary to the logical form, it is certainly false, because it contradicts itself.",[]],"busolt-s11-002":["Remark The dialectical use of the understanding is the production of illusion. The formal very often deceives. E.g. A beautiful garment adorns a stupid head. On the other hand under a poor garment a scholar is often hidden, just so it is with cognitions. If of two opposed propositions the one is false, then the other is true. Whoever therefore wants to show the truth of a proposition has two ways before him. For one he can directly prove the truth of a proposition itself the other time the falsity of the opposed one, which latter happens when he correctly concludes from it something absurd. For a proposition is certainly false if it has false sequels. In the first case it is called a direct, in the other an indirect *et* apagogic demonstration. The *Kriteria* of truth are either essential or extra-essential. Essential they are when they rest in the cognition itself and harmonize with the supreme laws of the understanding. Extra-essential, when they consist in the agreement of the universal human understanding. The marks which we have of truth are therefore twofold:",[]],"busolt-s11-003":["1.) *a priori* and",[]],"busolt-s11-004":["2.) *a posteriori*.",[]],"busolt-s11-005":["The judgment *a posteriori*, whether a proposition which I have made according to universal rules of the understanding, also finds approval with everyone's understanding one cannot do without. It is the test whether a proposition accords with the common laws of the understanding, or not Marks are not objective but subjective, they are taken from the concurrence of others, of particular subjects. There are therefore objective *Kriteria* of truth, these consist in the agreement of cognition with the laws of the understanding and of reason. These are not sufficient. There are two subjective *Kriteria* of truth, which must come in addition to the previous ones, and consist in this, that one takes together the judgment of various people. I concern myself therefore here with the agreement of subjects. –",[]],"busolt-s11-006":["I must make a test:",[]],"busolt-s11-007":["1.) I compare my knowledge which I have of objects with the cognition of other objects which have already been accepted as true. This makes a great difference in the application of the common rules of the understanding and of reason.",[]],"busolt-s11-008":["2.) I see whether the cognition of my subject agrees with the cognition of other subjects.",[]],"busolt-s11-009":["In general that is true which holds for everyone, and what has only a private validity is only an illusion. The drive in the investigation of truth to consult others is already attached to the human being by nature. The formal essential knowledge [*Kenntniße*] of truth are:",[]],"busolt-s11-010":["a.) A knowledge [*Kenntniß*] must be possible this is a negative criterion.",[]],"busolt-s11-011":["b.) A cognition must in a certain way be grounded:",[]],"busolt-s11-012":["A.) As sequel and",[]],"busolt-s11-013":["B.) As ground. As such when it is a correct sequel from true grounds. As ground, when it is a ground of true sequels.",[]],"busolt-s11-014":["The sequels can be regarded as grounds (*rationes*), not indeed as *rationes succedendi*, but *cognoscendi*. The possibility of cognition must be connected with a true ground which has correct sequels, and with true sequels which have a correct ground. – Groundlessness and impossibility are signs of falsity. Groundlessness is an incomplete sign of falsity. – Impossibility is a sufficient, apodictic ground that a cognition is false On the other hand with truth possibility is a complete sign, but groundedness a sufficient sign of the same.",[]],"busolt-s11-015":["Truth is a perfection of cognition in general. It belongs however *specifice* to logical cognition. It is logically also aesthetically perfect, but especially aesthetically: for it relates to the subject.",[]],"busolt-s11-016":["Logical and aesthetic truth differ in this; aesthetic truth is that which agrees with the ends of sensibility. One of these two can take place in a cognition, but the other not. E.g. One criticizes in Shakespeare that he lets the ghosts appear. There is here not indeed logical truth, but still aesthetic for Shakespeare has the end of impressing this properly on the senses. Further it is indeed also nothing impossible that spirits could appear to us sensibly, although it is not settled. For these aims of Shakespeare however aesthetic truth is sufficient E.g. in Aesop's fables the animals are presented speaking. We can think a cognition which is wholly true, also one which is wholly false, but also one which is neither true nor false. Every judgment, even if it is false, must still in some respect accord with the laws of the understanding, and also be somewhat true. It is thus certain that in a false judgment there is always something little true, even if it is inconsiderable compared to the false. The author gives a distinction between an exact and a crude cognition. This distinction is incorrect. For to the crude the subtle cognition is opposed. An exact cognition is one which agrees with the object in all pieces, and is in conformity with the end. It is called a precise cognition. A subtle one in which there is a greater degree of cognition than the end requires, a crude cognition, in which there is a smaller degree of cognition than the end requires. E.g. The cognition of God is an exact, crude also subtle cognition. All human beings must have a correct cognition of God. The degree of correctness is different in different human beings. A teacher e.g. must already have a subtle cognition. But if he thought him as a corporeal being i.e. if he had a crude cognition, then he would therefore be much to be blamed. Even if it is permitted to the common man. Subtle cognition errs in the extensive. There are sciences which require everything precisely E.g. I go walking; then it is better if I give the money which I want to spend on it to the poor, thereby I exercise my duty of love. One says: something is *tolerabiliter* true i.e. when the fault in the cognition is not considerable; or when the incorrect [element] of a cognition does not hinder its end. The author makes in § 104 a distinction between dogmatic and historical truths (*ex datis*) these are opposed to the cognitions of reason (*ex principiis*); but not the dogmatic ones. Cognition of reason is from concepts, and is an apodictic ground, i.e. which with the consciousness of necessity",[]],"busolt-s11-017":["is combined. *Ex Principiis* a cognition is either from concepts, or from construction of concepts. The cognitions from concepts are *dogmata* but those which are combined with intuition, i.e. when the universal is cognized in intuition (*in concreto*) are *Mathemata*. The first is thus a philosophical, the other a mathematical cognition. Now to the author a multitude of dogmatic judgments are truths which are so combined that they make up a whole, a system. But this it is not. For one can take the manifold together through mere similarity. A system however is where the idea of the whole precedes the determination of the parts. An aggregate however is where the knowledge of the parts precedes the concept of the whole. The definition of our author of system would thus be nothing other than an aggregate Aggregate I can according to similarity; not according to ideas. The author says: That a system consists of dogmatic truths, this is not right for there are indeed also other systems e.g. the system of nature is a historical system. All cognition which makes up an aggregate can be called a rhapsody, but that which makes up a system, a science, i.e. concept of the whole where namely the parts of the same taken together make up a whole. Truth is opposed to error. Error is a representation of a false cognition as a truth. We do not always make use of the laws of the understanding and of reason. How can error arise? This is a difficult question. We therefore note:",[]],"busolt-s11-018":["1.) Error and truth take place only in reason, not in concepts, or intuitions.",[]],"busolt-s11-019":["2.) Through the senses we have no judgments; but only intuition. Where there is no judging, there is neither truth nor error. The senses therefore also do not err.",[]],"busolt-s11-020":["3.) The understanding also does not err with concepts",[]],"busolt-s11-021":["A compendious means of not erring is: not to judge at all. This means is surely not to be recommended, but nevertheless it is good, when the cognition of the matter is [not] too much elevated above our cognition of the understanding; because from that an error very easily arises. One can judge, and yet not err, namely when one does not judge decisively, not assertorically; but problematically; when I add to my judgment: *non liquet*, it is not settled. But to judge thus is no gain for reason. I must only at the beginning judge the cognition problematically, in time assertorically. Problematic judging must precede the assertoric. Those who judge problematically one calls doubters; better one could call them *cunctatores*.",[]],"busolt-s11-022":["All judgments are actions of the understanding and of reason although they are also erroneous, still we cannot say that the understanding in itself errs. For it is the touchstone of truth. The understanding acts according to its laws always concordantly, and the concordance is formal truth. The senses do not err in themselves. For they do not judge. Error springs from the mixing of both. We do not namely distinguish the inference of the senses from the effects of the understanding. A force can only deviate from its laws; when another interferes. E.g. I let a stone fall from the tower; then I will judge it will fall straight down, but it is driven to the side by the wind; then I will still not think that it has deviated of itself, but that a second force has mixed in, which pushed it along. Just so it is with the understanding and with the senses. We appeal in error to the limitations of reason and thereby insult not ourselves; but nature. This is however wrong: for the narrow limitations of reason are the causes of lack and not of error Every error arises partly from the understanding, partly from the senses, we must therefore seek the sources of the same in both. Insofar as it has now sprung from the understanding, it is true. Error also sometimes springs from the understanding, thus all error, as a judgment, has a partial truth. When subjective grounds are falsely taken for objective ones, then this is an error we confuse the subjective with the constitution of the object, and then an error arises, through this exchange. One here exchanges the conditions of the understanding with the conditions of sensibility. A negative criterion of truth is thus the certainty that one has not erred, that one has separated off the subjective grounds of taking to be true. One has made use merely of the understanding with suspension of sensibility. That however occurs seldom. – For one cannot notice it, it is like an enchantment, when one mixes in sensibility. E.g. When someone tells a superstitious little story; he says beforehand, I am not superstitious, and yet finds the little story wondrous. Meanwhile one must guard oneself in every possible way against the influence of the understanding on sensibility, e.g. All lying is shameful, but one says emergency lies are permitted. But this they are not. For for the most part this happens from self-interest; this is an influence of sensibility in the use of the understanding, and here lying is just as disgraceful as otherwise. In every science such an intermixture takes place, except in mathematics, where I see everything *intuitu*. The author speaks of a certain unavoidable error (*error invincibilis*) which as certainly since a certain ignorance is unavoidable. E.g. When the last day will come. An error is always avoidable, the best aid is, not to judge. But when I must judge something e.g. in the things of religion, then in this case sometimes an error is unavoidable. In morals one must judge, but there no error is unavoidable: for it has a certain *euidenz* with it. In judging the things of nature e.g. whether there are inhabitants in the moon, there can be an unavoidable error, but I may not judge here. We have in all things where one does not know the absolutely great a mean measure, what is greater than this is admired, what is smaller is despised. Just so it is with the understanding: The common understanding is the mean measure between all human beings' understanding, and one calls that absurd which contradicts this understanding. To deny human beings common understanding is an insult. Error can often be refuted through a test. – But one must seek to get to the causes and constitution of the error. No error is cured by being refuted: but by being explained, since otherwise one insults the honor of the reason of others. All truths are either necessary or contingent. – The necessary must be seen *a priori* through reason, but the contingent through experience. – Both are considered by logic, even though the rules of logic are necessary ones Mathematics has necessary truths, and judges *a priori*. Exercise and test however do not confirm the proposition; but serve for the explanation of these necessary truths, which one calls apodictic truths.",[]],"busolt-s12-001":["A mark signifies a partial concept of a whole representation. It does not consider a part of the thing. By mark Wolff wrongly understands a concept of distinction: for 2 things can after all also have a similarity. There is thus a mark of *identitaet*, and one of *Diuersitaet*; to cognize identity, for that our understanding is already disposed of itself; to cognize diversity it must be driven. The marks are",[]],"busolt-s12-002":["1.) Mediate i.e. a mark of a mark, a mark of a representation of the mark.",[]],"busolt-s12-003":["2.) Immediate, i.e. a mark of the object which does not arise through universal concepts e.g. A body would be represented to me and I say it is a stone, then one asks what is a stone? It is a body which consists of very hard earth. What is earth? What is body? This is mediate. The first one calls subordinated the other coordinated, the first make up a series, the others an aggregate, and are partial representations which belong as *partes* to the whole. In the series of the subordinated a mark *a priori* must be a first mark E.g. What is a human being? a thinking being in an organic body: What is thinking? Representing through concepts. What is representing? Further I cannot go, this is the first mark, and such are universal. The marks of the coordinated are a line without boundary but they are countable in which they make up a concept which they have of the thing. There are secondly negating and affirming marks. The former properly is no partial concept of a thing, it is a relative the other a subjective mark. The negative ones are nothing other than representations for avoiding error. There are sciences where the negative marks make up the greatest part e.g. natural theology. Often one can have a negative mark of a thing, but a positive one not. There are further important and slight, fruitful and unfruitful, sufficient and insufficient marks. Sufficient marks are those which have the whole concept of the thing. It must hold of the identity.",[]],"busolt-s12-004":["The marks are either internal or external Internal, which consider the internal constitution and properties, the sum total of all internal marks insofar as they make up a concept is an internal concept. All internal marks are either *notae primitiuae* which belong to one thing alone or *notae deriuatiuae*, where more than one mark can be posited of a thing.",[]],"busolt-s12-005":["The logical essence is *complexus omnium conceptuum primitiuorum*. Primitive marks are *essentialia*. the derivative marks",[]],"busolt-s12-006":["are *attributa*. The *essentialia logica* are also called *constitutiua* and are regarded as grounds. They are the *Attributa rationalia*, which come from the grounds constituent signs, when they do not as sequels of other marks belong to the essence of things. E.g. The three sides of the triangle are *essentialien*, they belong to the essence; the angles are *attributen*. E.g. In a body extension and shape are as *constitutiua*. They belong to the essence and are *primitiua*, on the other hand divisibility, mobility, alterability, are *deriuatiua*. *Extraessentialien* are either no internal marks at all and are *relationes* or they are indeed internal, but they are *modi* e.g. that a human being is rich or poor, master or servant are *relationes*. Erudition is a *modus*. Clear cognitions belong to logic, but obscure ones not, rather to psychology. That clear cognition is in us, we can know through truth, but that obscure is in us, through inferences. Logic has nothing at all to do with obscure representations, clear ones must be given to it. The action by which a concept becomes clear, when it was previously obscure, is called *euolutio conceptus*. The making distinct of a representation is not *euolutio*; but *analysis*, or dissection. Analysis of a concept is the concept successively led onto the partial representations. Of the first logic does not provide us means, but of the other. When the marks become clear; then the whole representation becomes distinct. In the concept of the triangle I have",[]],"busolt-s12-007":["1.) The concept of a surface",[]],"busolt-s12-008":["2.) Of a flat surface",[]],"busolt-s12-009":["3.) Of an enclosed surface; and in this way I have a distinct representation of a triangle.",[]],"busolt-s12-010":["The author speaks in § 125 of objective and speculative obscurity; but the obscure is always subjective; for the object of my consideration comprehends within itself neither distinctness nor obscurity, but rather this arises merely through the representation or the concepts of some thing to obscure representations we very often have much to be thankful for. For, in that we would very often have been plunged through speculative reason into artificial sophistical errors, if some obscure representations had not still held us back; obscure representations are thus not to be regarded as none at all, but as such as do not leave behind for us a sufficient concept of an object. *Completudo conceptus clari* [completeness of a clear concept] consists in the totality of the marks which, taken together, form the concept of a thing. These concepts we encounter little in empirical things. Total clarity will be only there where we can also connect what we think with intuition. Subjective clarity of cognition is strength and liveliness of the understanding. All passions one can surely arouse in jest, only not disgust this is quickly aroused, but does not vanish so soon: for the other passions enliven the powers of the mind, but disgust strikes them down. Strength is distinguished from liveliness in this, that it is a liveliness of practical consequences. Liveliness is something when a thing is brought to intuition for us as if the thing were actually present before me. But there is also a liveliness which is not even objectively sensible. If a thing is presented in a lively way then it must",[]],"busolt-s12-011":["1.) Lead to interest, this is objective",[]],"busolt-s12-012":["2.) Act upon intuition, and that is subjective",[]],"busolt-s12-013":["To make a concept distinct does not mean to extend a concept; but rather to make it distinct in all its parts through analysis, but if I want to know distinctly the object of which I do not yet have a distinct concept, then this means to make it distinct through synthesis, or to add on that which intuition offers to cognition. If, e.g., I know that gold is not only yellow, malleable and of particular weight, but also that it does not rust and is fire-resistant; then my concept of gold is extended *per synthesin* [through synthesis]. Distinctness *per synthesin* can come about empirically, i.e., through experience or *a posteriori*.",[]],"busolt-s12-014":["One can represent things in various ways.",[]],"busolt-s12-015":["1.) To represent something to oneself through a concept.",[]],"busolt-s12-016":["2.) To know through concepts",[]],"busolt-s12-017":["3.) To cognize through the understanding, or to understand",[]],"busolt-s12-018":["4.) Through insight through rules *a priori*.",[]],"busolt-s12-019":["5.) To have insight into something sufficiently with respect to all things.",[]],"busolt-s13-001":["Extensive magnitude rests on the distinctness of coordinated marks, as an aggregate; intensive magnitude rests on the distinctness of subordinated marks or of the marks of marks. Extensive distinctness is *completudo cognitionis* [completeness of cognition], intensive distinctness is *Profunditas cognitionis* [profundity of cognition]. *Profunditas* or thoroughness is the series of subordinated marks, or of the marks of marks. To make one's cognition perfect negatively does not mean to aggregate cognition; but only to keep off that which would be a hindrance to cognition. Precision is when the marks are substituted for the thing. If the concept neither has too few marks, or is complete, nor comprehends too many marks within itself, or is precise, then it is *conceptus, qui adaequat rem* [a concept that is adequate to the thing].",[]],"busolt-s13-002":["Remark. Precision one cannot demand in all sciences; but *completudinem* [completeness] throughout. If, then, a concept is to be adequate to a thing, it must first be complete and afterwards precise. From this precision follows the elegance which mathematics possesses. – For when the mathematician gives a definition; this must be so appropriate to the thing that no rhetorical beauties occur in it. Precision is thus in the strictest sense a logical perfection, abundance must have a permission, where it is to be admitted, and this can be given only there where something happens *in fauorem sensualem* [in favor of the sensible].",[]],"busolt-s14-001":["Certainty is a sufficient consciousness of truth. It is thus a state of our reason. Taking to be true signifies the state of the mind insofar as it judges of a thing. Certainty is objective. This is certainty:",[]],"busolt-s14-002":["1.) Empirical and",[]],"busolt-s14-003":["2.) Rational through reason, or apodictically certain. The various kinds of taking to be true are:",[]],"busolt-s14-004":["1.) Opining, i.e.: when I take a thing to be true, although I know that I have no sufficient ground for it. It is neither a subjective nor an objective ground of taking to be true. It is asked whether we here judge assertorically or problematically? The answer is:",[]],"busolt-s14-005":["It is properly nothing other than a problematic taking to be true. Opinions are not determinate declarations. It is a sign that a human being thinks if he can alter his opinions, if he knows how to consider them from another side. Opinion is properly",[]],"busolt-s14-006":["a consciousness of the insufficiency of one's own taking to be true.",[]],"busolt-s14-007":["Opining is of great use. All knowledge except the mathematical begins from opinions, after opinion comes the comparison of marks and then certainty.",[]],"busolt-s14-008":["2.) Believing, this is a subjectively sufficient ground of taking to be true, with the consciousness that it is objectively insufficient. Opining is neither a subjective nor an objective, believing only a subjective sufficient ground of taking to be true. This believing is no objective necessity for the reason that the other can have just as weighty grounds for not taking the thing to be true. At the beginning we must also judge with insufficient grounds: For they are a part of certainty.",[]],"busolt-s14-009":["3.) Knowing. It is a subjectively sufficient taking to be true, with the certainty that it is also objectively sufficient. Believing we restrict to another's testimony; on this we can also already ground knowing. Where there are not documents, there we only believe. But even if I have quite distinct characteristics, I still cannot say, I know it certainly: for there can still be something insufficient. Best is the language of modesty, that one says: I believe it. In believing there always runs in along with the speculative cognition a practical interest, that is of such a kind that no human being can get it out. The ground of taking to be true through belief is often more sufficient than knowing. Remark. In opining, I judge problematically, in believing assertorically, in knowing apodictically. The objectively sufficient taking to be true, although it becomes as logically insufficient, is sufficient for belief in cognition without regard to the act of logical taking to be true. Here the talk is of historical belief. There is also a rational belief. If practical taking to be true is necessary in a certain aim; then it is a rational belief. It is wondrous that a rational belief can take place, but through reason a human being can indeed believe that there is a God. In speculative or philosophical cognitions one can opine, but only of empirical things. Now if there are things which merely concern reason; then I cannot opine, I must have grounds either to believe or to know. In empirical things we can pass opinions, because, if we pursue these, we go forward on the guiding thread of insufficient grounds and also perhaps gradually come to certainty, and can confirm it. E.g., Of the thunderstorm one at first believed that it could have a similarity with electricity, and afterwards one confirmed it through experience.",[]],"busolt-s14-010":["In mathematics we have not opinions; but certainty. In philosophy opinions can also take place; in order to refute the opinions of others. Believing properly holds only of practical cognitions. One also sometimes uses it of empirical ones, e.g., Whether the moon has inhabitants; certainty is either empirical or rational. Empirically I can be certain of a thing that it exists, not that it is necessary. To the latter belongs a rational certainty where reason settles something, there experience is slight. E.g., If someone, after he had proved a proposition, that all angles in a triangle make up 180 degrees, wanted to take the protractor and measure it. Rational certainty is either apodictic or moral. A speculative certainty is apodictic, a practical one moral. Apodictic is the relation of cognition to the understanding. Moral is the relation to the will. Moral certainty is that which I can assert with all conscientiousness, apodictic logical certainty is either, a Intuitive, i.e., mathematical certainty, or, b Discursive, i.e., philosophical certainty.",[]],"busolt-s14-011":["This can have apodictic certainty, but not intuition. Apodictic certainty is a complete certainty *a priori*, mathematical and philosophical certainties agree in this, that they are apodictic certainties; the difference lies in this, that the one is intuitive, the other discursive. Mathematical certainty carries with it a certain evidence *a priori*.",[]],"busolt-s14-012":["If cognition is objectively not sufficient, then it is a persuasion. Many a one believes himself convinced when he is persuaded. Whoever lets himself be easily persuaded indicates a weakness of the understanding. One makes use of the expressions accepting and rejecting.",[]],"busolt-s14-013":["They seem to refer to the will, and also actually refer to it: Accepting holds only there where there is no apodictic certainty. If I am certain of a thing, if the grounds are sufficient, then I must accept it. In order to accept something I need not exactly be certain, and then I have accepted it with a restriction. In philosophy such acceptances are very good, and are a postponement, *suspension* of judgment. This suspension is either critical or skeptical. A critical suspension is: when I postpone my judgments in order to pass them in future. In such a suspension I say: *non liquet* [it is not clear]. A skeptical suspension is; when I always leave the thing in obscurity.",[]],"busolt-s14-014":["Of such a suspension one says better that he gives up his judgment, he renounces it, than that he suspends it.",[]],"busolt-s14-015":["The dogmatists settle the thing at once, and do not suspend their judgment. Many a one is by nature dogmatic, commonly the proud, who have a great self-conceit. But they are also sometimes struck down again by the skeptics, who also have their self-conceit, in that they always renounce judgment. To postpone one's judgment is useful in philosophy, and it is good if one accustoms oneself to it early. One must not at once close one's soul. But when it comes down to the practical, one must be resolute. We have two kinds of judgment,",[]],"busolt-s14-016":["a. A provisional one",[]],"busolt-s14-017":["b. A determinate one.",[]],"busolt-s14-018":["Judging provisionally is at all times, however few grounds we may have for one or the other side. E.g., When a human being enters a company, everyone at once passes a judgment from his features upon his character. When a human being looks pale one is accustomed at once to infer his sickliness. But it is faulty if one takes a provisional judgment for a determinate one for illusion can deceive. Provisional judgments lead us to determinate ones. Provisional judgments can also already be correct, and since out of impatience one does not apply them to determinate ones, but takes them themselves for such, there arises a principle, and that one calls prejudice. From such rational provisional judgments arise the proverbs and canons of the school, these one must not take for determinate judgments.",[]],"busolt-s14-019":["We have subjective grounds of judgment, better one could call them causes of judgment, if we falsely take these subjective grounds for objective ones, so that a *vitium subreptionis* [fault of subreption] arises. To this can contribute:",[]],"busolt-s14-020":["a.) Opinion, predilection, i.e., that everything seems to us worthy of approval, and even if it is evil, nevertheless deserves excuse.",[]],"busolt-s14-021":["There is also a predilection which is an *esprit du Corps*, since one takes everything to be true; further there is a predilection of the fatherland, and predilection of property.",[]],"busolt-s14-022":["b.) Habit, this is very bad, since such people who possess it are convinced even by the most distinct proofs only through length of time. Such is found in all people, chiefly those who do not gladly deviate from their concept. One must refrain from accustoming oneself to nothing, i.e., from making it into a necessity.",[]],"busolt-s14-023":["One is also accustomed to call that a prejudice which flows from prejudice, and so an actual prejudice is a principle. A prejudice is a principle from positive understanding, or reason. The understanding or reason consists in activity, is active, has a spontaneity, the senses are passive. In prejudice reason is only suffering, for it arises from inclination, this is in itself passive. We can thus also have reason in a passive state. We have two acts of the spontaneity of the understanding.",[]],"busolt-s14-024":["a.) Reflexion or reflection, when we compare our judgment with the laws of the understanding.",[]],"busolt-s14-025":["b.) Investigation, when I seek at once to come to the ground of judgments Certain judgments require an investigation, but many not But reflection must take place in every judgment, namely whether it holds subjectively or objectively.",[]],"busolt-s14-026":["Sometimes we judge without reflection, we namely do not reflect whether the judgment springs from the understanding, i.e., is objective, or whether the senses have been inserted, i.e., subjective, whereby inclination has slipped something in, and hence it can easily come about that human beings take subjective causes for objective ones, and then arises prejudice is a propensity to take subjective causes for objective ones; A prejudice is found nowhere other than where there is a propensity, it is *propensio ad iudicia ex caußis subiectiuis* [a propensity to judgments from subjective causes]. Such a prejudice arises from imitation, inclination, habit. Imitation takes place not only in children; but also in older people. To this belong the proverbs of the rabble, sentences of learned people and poets, the canons of the school.",[]],"busolt-s14-027":["Precipitancy is a lack of reflection of the understanding Prejudice comes about through a propensity, not through precipitancy. The propensity that has arisen from new habit makes prejudices into necessity. There are",[]],"busolt-s14-028":["a. Prejudices of authority. This is grounded on the high esteem that we have for others. Authority holds either for the person, for the high esteem that we have for the multitude, and for what all the world says, it is good. Further we also ground on it a correctness. The judgment of the multitude is a criterion of truth: For since in the multitude there are various subjects, but the judgment of the multitude must be subjective, i.e., that they must all agree in one, so we accept the judgment as correct.",[]],"busolt-s14-029":["b.) The prejudice of antiquity. From the end of the previous century until now the prejudice of novelties went on.",[]],"busolt-s14-030":["But now one wants again to extol the old writings. Fontenelle has published a writing on ancient and modern writers, in it he says: that we may give the ancients no preference: for we are all made of one material. He says, We have yet the advantage over them in this, that we have their writings as preliminary exercises which they would not have had. We have a prejudice of antiquity:",[]],"busolt-s14-031":["a.) Because we believe the sciences decline with the years. Nature becomes older and worsens.",[]],"busolt-s14-032":["b.) We take the knowledge of the ancients for erudition and thereby esteem antiquity itself highly. But in historical things we have much to thank the ancients for.",[]],"busolt-s14-033":["c.) Our astonishment we take for an admiration. Astonishment concerns sensibility, since we do not expect something. Admiration concerns the object itself, namely the excellent. Just so is it with antiquity, we are namely astonished that the ancients already thought so; In a learned woman we are astonished at her erudition, because it is something rare, but we do not admire it. This astonishment is very near to the impression of admiration.",[]],"busolt-s14-034":["d.) Our high esteem for antiquity comes from this, because the books of the ancients that are still left to us, these so few products, escaped the destructive force; so we believe that what has preserved itself through so long a time must certainly be the best.",[]],"busolt-s14-035":["Remark The favorable prejudice of antiquity reigns especially among the *literati*, as those who properly occupy themselves with reading of the ancient writers. – These now believe they already find new discoveries in the writings of the ancients. But commonly they do not find them before they have already been discovered. Thus Aristarchus is supposed already to have had the Copernican system, and in the same way one seeks to make demonstrable from the word *versorum* the ancients' knowledge of magnetic force.",[]],"busolt-s14-036":["e.) Gratitude toward the ancients is likewise a ground of the favorable prejudice for them, and they still always remain in truth the heroes of erudition and the first pioneers. Thus in the philosophical Aristotle, in the mathematical Euclid, Archimedes are always the fathers, so in the models of taste, and of the popularity reigning in them, they will always remain our masters. We thus owe them much and can therefore surely assess them; But not condemn them; although one, especially in our modern times, drives this obligation too far, in that in the learning of the sciences one proposes the way of *lecturae* [reading] more than that of reflecting. To be sure, wide reading in the ancient writings is to be recommended, but in doing so one must not abandon the way of reflecting.",[]],"busolt-s14-037":["1.) The envy of the learned among each other: for just as the learned have their power to thank merely to the ill will of the books, in that, as it were out of long weariness of the long pomp, they transfer dominion to one alone, which makes them all equal, so it goes in regard to the ancients.",[]],"busolt-s14-038":["2.) The prejudice of novelty. Here we can believe that we will bring it further than the ancients.",[]],"busolt-s14-039":["3.) The prejudice of the adopted doctrinal edifice. Those sciences that are grounded on a dogmatic doctrinal edifice are not free of such prejudices, likewise philosophy. Such a prejudice is in which we, even through the best objective grounds, will accept nothing that is against our system.",[]],"busolt-s14-040":["4.) Egoism. This is",[]],"busolt-s14-041":["a.) Moral, i.e., since one holds oneself alone of worth, but not others.",[]],"busolt-s14-042":["b.) Logical, since one holds one's own judgment as decisive, but all other judgments on the contrary as superfluous and their contradiction as indifferent. One pays no heed at all to agreeing judgments.",[]],"busolt-s14-043":["5.) The prejudice of sensibility, i.e., a kind of laziness: for one gives the common understanding a preference over all sciences, one believes one settles everything through the common understanding. One does not properly distinguish the popular use of the understanding from the scientific. A science also falls under suspicion if it wholly contradicts the common understanding. These prejudices cited were prejudices of trust.",[]],"busolt-s14-044":["The prejudice of mistrust is when one is prejudiced against antiquity, or against novelty. Besides these there is also found a certain melancholy way of thinking, which is commonly accustomed to set in little by little through increasing experience. It is twofold.",[]],"busolt-s14-045":["a.) in the practical, that is in regard to the morals of human beings and in regard to actions. of this, e.g., the historians can impart to us the very best ideas, when in all ages we become aware of so assiduous an effort of mortals to harm one another; so we finally become mistrustful toward all human beings, and banish fidelity and honesty from the whole civilized world. This one calls misanthropy.",[]],"busolt-s14-046":["b.) In the theoretical, in regard to philosophy, that is called misology or a hatred against reason; since namely learned people, after they have given themselves so much trouble, still finally see themselves unsatisfied in the most important and most necessary questions, for which their reason does not suffice. E.g., Whence am I? What will I be after death? A Condamine, a Rousseau can give examples of this. This prejudice arises for the most part from this, because one at first represented to oneself all too much. There is further found a prejudice against the nationality. It is a common prejudice, and takes place chiefly among the French. The German nation on the other hand does not have it, it rather has a propensity to imitate others. There is also a prejudice of mistrust against oneself. This is something rare, in that one more often trusts oneself more rather than less. Nevertheless it takes place, and indeed in persons who could trust themselves more. Probability *Probabilitas est dimidium certitudinis; possibilis modus id est, quod gradum quendam probabilitatis habet* [probability is the half of certainty; the possible mode, that is, that which has a certain degree of probability]. It pertains to objective grounds. *Verisimilitudo* [verisimilitude], however, to subjective ones. Although in all of these there are insufficient grounds, they are still always grounds of taking to be true. All probabilities are a relation of truth to certainty, consequently here the ground must be compared with sufficient grounds. An objective criterion of probability there cannot be, honesty and sincerity",[]],"busolt-s14-047":["of the witnesses seems indeed to be an objective criterion of truth, but is not. but rather it only makes us attentive to the criteria of probability, therefore they give us no rules at all according to which we can assess the probable. The assessing of probability is not a work of the understanding; but of the power of judgment; this shows us whether it stands under the rules of the understanding, or not. All observations that one makes differ among themselves thus both *in exceßu* [in excess] and *in defectu* [in defect]. – The mean of all these observations can be taken for the most probable. To specify the rules of probability without distinction is wholly impossible.",[]],"busolt-s14-048":["Doubt is a subjectively insufficient ground that contradicts taking to be true. It is the means whereby truth is weakened, but also at the same time an *Organon* of its strengthening. When doubts are refuted, when they are removed, there arise from their removal again doubts whether it is also actually a step to a nearer insight into truth or not. Hereby I now strengthen myself in the grounds of refutation which flow from the doubted truth, consequently I will also bring this nearer. Both in taking to be true and in objections I begin with obscure representations. Before we make objections, taking to be true begins to become doubtful, one does not want to concede this or that, but can still not make it known to the other, and so a scruple arises. A scruple is thus a ground of doubt insofar as it is not clearly cognized, and takes place chiefly in those who cannot bring their objections to distinctness. One finds scruples from subjective causes. These cannot be refuted, for they concern the state of mind, habit. With an objection we have to conduct ourselves twofold:",[]],"busolt-s14-049":["a.) When one rejects it,",[]],"busolt-s14-050":["b.) When one answers it. One calls a proposition that has not been doubted a settled truth. This remains correct even after long doubts and retains a certainty. In human reason we can have subjective grounds in order to assert a thing objectively. These latter are principles Skepticism denies reason all certainty, and holds it as uncertain. The skeptic says: There are no objective assertions. But precisely by saying it he contradicts himself. Nothing can be determined as certain, is their universal principle. Now, however, they do determine something when they say: that each one has only subjective assertions. One calls a certain resolution maxim, this is a subjectively practical principle of reason, i.e., a decision in regard to the object to make use of one's reason in a certain way E.g., I will never lie even if I could gain very much thereby: Every method requires certain subjective rules according to which one combines cognitions, and a human being without maxims has no reliability at all. The maxims of reflecting are:",[]],"busolt-s14-051":["a.) The maxims of dogmatic use, i.e., the resolution to make use of one's reason in such a way that one hopes to attain apodictic certainty. *Maxima confidentia in certitudinem cognitionis humanae est dogmatica* [the greatest confidence in the certainty of human cognition is dogmatic].",[]],"busolt-s14-052":["b.) The maxims of skeptical use, since one always doubts of coming to certainty *Maxima diffidentia in certitudinem cognitionis humanae est sceptica* [the greatest diffidence in the certainty of human cognition is skeptical]. Of both kinds there have been learned men. The skeptics also called themselves *Zeteticos* [zetetics]. Of both kinds one proved the groundlessness of the other. The school of Plato was called *academia*, of this there were 3 schools, of which the second fell into skepticism. Arcesilaus was its founder. There is also another school of skeptics who called themselves Pyrrhonists. They distinguished themselves from the Academics by this, that the latter asserted that there is no truth at all; the former posited only a doubt therein, they took only a postponement. There is an unrestricted trust, also an unrestricted mistrust toward human reason. *Katexochen* dogmatic is he who has an unrestricted trust from concepts for human reason. The opposite *katexochen* skeptical. The skeptical maxim wholly removes all use of the employment of reason etc. The dogmatic seems at first to be of great use, but afterwards one finds it otherwise. The critic is between both. This critical use of reason is to be recommended among the cited kinds of use of reason, because it then only judges when one has sufficiently examined the sources. Critique is always something permitted, indeed even necessary, because human cognition can come to sufficient certainty merely thereby. Only a pity that this freedom of critique is so greatly misused. Critique is not used before the understanding has come to a certain maturity.",[]],"busolt-s15-001":["A hypothesis is the taking to be true of a cognition, since I infer from the sufficiency of the same to its true consequences, e.g., When I know that there is fire under the earth. The cognition, then, from which many true consequences flow is a hypothesis. If a ground has nothing but true consequences; then it is a true ground, but I do not yet cognize it *a priori*; but *a posteriori*, or through a hypothesis. Hypothesis is a presupposition. A ground that has nothing but true consequences ceases to be a true opinion, it becomes certain. A hypothesis can never be brought to apodictic certainty. E.g., The system of Copernicus is only hypothesis. From given consequences we cannot infer with security to determinate grounds, unless it can also be shown that the consequences flow from a single ground. –",[]],"busolt-s15-002":["To the perfection of a hypothesis belongs the following:",[]],"busolt-s15-003":["a.) The possibility of the hypothesis must be certain: Apodictically certain is no hypothesis. From this it follows that in metaphysics there are no hypotheses, in physics on the other hand they are necessary.",[]],"busolt-s15-004":["b.) The consequence of the consequences derived from this assumed ground must be certain.",[]],"busolt-s15-005":["c.) The sufficiency of the assumed ground for all possible consequences remains uncertain.",[]],"busolt-s15-006":["d.) There belongs to it the unity of the assumed ground, which makes up the whole probable of the hypothesis. – The more subsidiary hypotheses are needed, the more improbable is the hypothesis. To infer from the consequences to the grounds gives an insecurity, because precisely the same cognition can have more than one ground, and because one consequence flows from more than one ground. The correctness of such a ground one proves through the consequences, but one can never bring it to apodictic certainty.",[]],"busolt-s16-001":["Complete taking to be true with consciousness one calls conviction. Taking to be true from subjective grounds that are sufficient in practical relation is belief. Taking to be true of which one does not know whether it arises from subjective or objective grounds is persuasion. An orator chiefly has the aim to persuade. Approval is to spring forth, even if he knows of neither objective nor subjective sufficiency.",[]],"busolt-s16-002":["One distinguishes knowing and being able thereby; what we know that it should happen, but cannot carry out; so of the first holds knowing, of the other being able. properly, not every knowing conditions a science: for this makes up a system. Also not every being able is an art for this is the faculty of bringing something about according to ideas. In every science that makes up a system there is also art: for",[]],"busolt-s16-003":["1.) There is in every system one,",[]],"busolt-s16-004":["2.) It is always an art to bring something into systematic order.",[]],"busolt-s16-005":["The idea is the ground of the manifold in regard to the product of heads. The artist acts according to a system, the craftsman through imitation. The artist and the scholar also have similarity. Rhapsodies of cognitions can be very rich, and yet no system. This rests on systematic unity of the idea of the whole. For sciences there belongs convincing knowledge of the totality of the whole, and for this there must precede the idea of the whole, which determines in advance the place for each part. E.g., As at the beginning of the logic there was talk of matter and form, of canon and organon etc. etc. Just so is it with art, it comes from being able, and differs in this from craft. An exercise for which skill through imitation is required is craft, but through ideas art. E.g., Is a builder an artist? Even if he works according to a plan, then he is only a copyist, but he has an exercise according to the idea of another, which he must be able to grasp. This one calls a virtuoso, but he who has originality of ideas, i.e., who has his own genius, is an artist, and if he builds a house in such a way, he is an architect. One also acts according to ideas that cannot be taught they cannot be set out, and even if it were possible, the other could not at the same time act according to these rules. These are also arts, and not sciences, e.g., the art of reckoning, the art of poetry etc. etc. There are also arts where the sciences precede. E.g., Glass-grinding according to optics. What sciences may be? The sum total of cognition is either an aggregate or a system; these cognitions may be historical or rational cognitions. Both, aggregate as well as system, is a whole. A system is that whole whose combination rests on an idea. In the aggregate the parts precede the whole; but in the system the idea of the whole precedes the composition of the parts. Every sum total of cognitions is called a science; insofar as the cognitions make up a system; all historical cognition as well as all autodidaxy is always an aggregate and no system.",[]],"busolt-s17-001":["There is an apodictic and an empirical proof. One can sometimes prove something empirically but still not give it apodictic certainty, e.g., Whether the sea once stood over the dry land. To empirical proof is opposed rational proof *a priori*. – A proof that cannot come to apodictic certainty is an analogical proof. Both, the apodictic as well as the analogical proof, is either discursive or intuitive. The latter is proof of construction through concepts, the former through concepts, An intuitive apodictic proof can take place nowhere other than in mathematics; for:",[]],"busolt-s17-002":["1.) It is to be an apodictic proof *a priori*,",[]],"busolt-s17-003":["2.) To be cognized through intuition *a priori*.",[]],"busolt-s17-004":["To this belong not only concepts; but also construction of concepts, whereby one is capable of an intuiting concept, and this is *demonstration*. This can thus take place nowhere other than in mathematics. This apodictic certainty, whether it be discursive or intuitive; is called certainty of the first rank The empirical makes up that of the second rank. The author also speaks of arbitrary truth. If something is arbitrarily true; then I can take it as I will. – If something is accepted which is not objectively determined, then this is arbitrary truth of a proposition, and is called *position*. E.g., That we count from 1 to 10, that in a circle there are 360 degrees etc. etc. Now of proofs there is required",[]],"busolt-s17-005":["a.) The proved cognition",[]],"busolt-s17-006":["b.) the interconnection of the grounds of proof with the cognition,",[]],"busolt-s17-007":["c.) A certainty that consists of this interconnection.",[]],"busolt-s17-008":["Thus no immediately certain proposition is a proof. The form of the grounds rests on distinctness. This can now be intuitive or discursive. The distinctness of the interconnection of a cognition",[]],"busolt-s17-009":["or judgment with its grounds gives to the *demonstration* its discursive distinctness. If one considers a proof *generaliter* [in general]; then one can take the *probation* from various grounds. I can ground it empirically, this gives no apodictic certainty Every proof of reason must be complete. One here also distinguishes proof and ground of proof. Every ground of proof is an incomplete proof, and only the complete certainty, the distinctness of the interconnection with the grounds of proof makes the proof. An empirical proof shows the truth of the proposition from actuality, as such is encountered in other things. An apodictic or rational proof shows, from the constitution of the matter, the necessity. A proof is a distinct cognition of the correct interconnection of a proposition with sufficient grounds. A proof can therefore be false",[]],"busolt-s17-010":["a.) *In Materia* [in the matter].",[]],"busolt-s17-011":["b.) *In Consequentia* [in the consequence], which mostly happens with analogical proofs.",[]],"busolt-s17-012":["This *consequenz* is the interconnection of the grounds with the sequences. An apodictic proof is therefore either a philosophical *probation* or mathematical *demonstration*. an analogical proof sometimes seems to be apodictic, because many concepts are often hidden in a discursive cognition. The author speaks of a provable and unprovable cognition. The word unprovable is otherwise equivocal. For it also means groundless-unprovable. Such truths are principles, and are the immediate certainty of a thing. They are the last ground of all mediate ones and need no proof. The immediate independent certainties must necessarily contain in themselves the ground of the dependent ones. Proofs are further divided into",[]],"busolt-s17-013":["a.) Direct and",[]],"busolt-s17-014":["b.) Indirect proofs. The former are called ostensive and the other apagogic proofs.",[]],"busolt-s17-015":["The former is when one proves the truth of the proposition directly through important grounds the other when one proves the falsity of the opposite, this is grounded on this; every proposition, whose opposite is false, is true.",[]],"busolt-s17-016":["Apagogic proofs belong not so much to mathematics as to philosophy. A proof, which has a perfect certainty",[]],"busolt-s17-017":["is also called a *demonstration*. A *demonstration* should properly be an apodictic certainty, a certainty through reason, and indeed an intuitive one, *cum intuitu a priori* [with intuition *a priori*], for if it were *a posteriori*, then it would be empirical; all *demonstration* is therefore mathematical. Mania for demonstrating is the affectation of introducing *demonstrations* in philosophy. One also calls it mania for demonstrating, when one proves things for which no proofs are necessary. This is found chiefly in *discussions*. Logic presupposes a concept of experience. We prove *a posteriori* when we prove through experience, *a priori*, when through reason. Belief is no source of cognition. I can believe *generaliter* [in general], i.e., I believe a cognition, and not someone, this happens through reason. The necessary presupposition of a cognition with the practical interest is believing. We cannot believe; if there is no practical interest in it. To believe something is therefore possible",[]],"busolt-s17-018":["a.) Through mere reason, but also",[]],"busolt-s17-019":["b.) Through testimonies.",[]],"busolt-s17-020":["Here I believe someone and this is a historical belief: rational belief is to be distinguished from reasonable belief: For historical belief too must be reasonable. A reasonable belief is that which has sufficient grounds to accept something.",[]],"busolt-s17-021":["Can I believe historically *objects* of rational belief? If I already believe through reason; then it would be ridiculous to believe historically; but if our reason does not suffice, then we can believe others; yet only in mathematics, not in philosophy. On the testimony of others I can accept something not otherwise than historically, and this has experience for its *object*. But we can believe the pronouncements of God: for with the concept of God the concept of the most perfect understanding is at the same time combined. This believing nevertheless comes down to human belief: for we believe those who recorded the Bible. No one can therefore state anything other than his experience; nevertheless there is an acquisition of cognitions, where we also believe something on the testimony of others. This rests:",[]],"busolt-s17-022":["a.) On the correctness of the testimonies,",[]],"busolt-s17-023":["b.) On the sincerity of the witnesses, to say nothing more than actually happened.",[]],"busolt-s17-024":["The Greeks and Romans did not attend to this. E.g. Curtius is full of lies in the speeches which he put into people's mouths. This principle has rather only been introduced among the learned for a hundred and fifty years. The *object* of historical belief is therefore experience where I increase my own through the experience of others. We can have experience from others, we can also have experiences from ourselves. With the experiences of others we must look to the competence and to the standing of the witnesses. Unbelief we can divide into a rational unbelief, and historical unbelief. A rational unbeliever one could also call morally unbelieving: for reason in the end issues in morality. This one is very much to be censured, for he overturns all morals. The historical unbelief on the other hand not: For it has nothing other than a lack of insight into the credibility of the witnesses. The learned have more cause to believe historically, than the common man: For they ground themselves here on the reputation of the learned. Historical unbelief therefore shows either",[]],"busolt-s17-025":["a.) Ignorance or",[]],"busolt-s17-026":["b.) An evil heart in that one declares all human beings liars, and in a malicious way will not believe their writings. One here rejects all sincerity all reliability among human beings, and it is in a certain respect also unbelief of morality.",[]],"busolt-s18-001":["We have, as a theoretical, so also a practical cognition, the first represents how something is, the second how something ought to be; in the same way one distinguishes the speculative and pragmatic cognition. The latter must contain the incentives to action. Even pragmatic cognitions, and in general every representation, if it does not at the same time contain the impulse to actions, has the ground for speculation. Practical knowledge is not precisely at all times pragmatic. For it is not an impulse, that I ought to do it; but only precepts given for the case, if someone wanted to carry them out. That which the author says further on this in this chapter does not belong to logic. A theoretical cognition can in turn be pragmatic. E.g. Whether God is just, omnipresent etc. etc. If we know this, then we seek to act rightly.",[]],"busolt-s19-001":["Logic has to do merely with the understanding; *operationes mentis* [operations of the mind] were already divided correctly by the ancients, namely: *apprehensio simplex* [simple apprehension] i.e. *conceptus iudicium et ratiocinium* [concept, judgment and reasoning]. Every representation we attain through intuition, *per intuitum*, but *intuitus* is properly *repraesentatio singularis* [singular representation], *conceptus est repraesentatio generalis* [a concept is a general representation]. The *intuitus* in mathematics is universal:",[]],"busolt-s19-002":["For this *intuitus* happens *per constructionem conceptuum* [through the construction of concepts]. *Repraesentatio cuius mihi conscius sum, est perceptio.* [A representation of which I am conscious is a perception.]",[]],"busolt-s19-003":["*Repraesentatio obiective spectata est cognitio.* [A representation considered objectively is a cognition.]",[]],"busolt-s19-004":["*Repraesentatio subiectiue spectata est sensatio* [A representation considered subjectively is a sensation], sensation. Cognition is the agreement of a representation with the object. All cognition is either *intuitus* or *conceptus*. Otherwise one divides the *conceptus*:",[]],"busolt-s19-005":["a.) into *conceptum sensitiuum* [sensitive concept] or *empiricum* [empirical], where we arrive at something through intuition:",[]],"busolt-s19-006":["b.) into *conceptum intellectualem purum* [pure intellectual concept], when the representation rests merely on the understanding and this is *notio*.",[]],"busolt-s19-007":["The idea of the *conceptus rationis* [concept of reason], for which not even an object in experience can be given, the use of the notion on the other hand can be in experience, and also finds here in experience its objects. – *Perceptio obiectiue spectata* [perception considered objectively] is therefore *cognitio*. This is either sensible or intellectual. Sensible cognitions *a posteriori* are called empirical. *conceptus sensitiui* [sensitive concepts] are where the *object* is given through the senses. The senses properly give no *conceptus*, the understanding is not *intuitus* but the senses do give me the matter for the *conceptus*; on the other hand the *conceptus* themselves are a work of the understanding. For this is the faculty of cognizing through concepts. A *conceptus* can accordingly be *formaliter* [formally] a product of the understanding, *materialiter* [materially] a product of the senses.",[]],"busolt-s19-008":["Remark of the senses properly not product but result is used, because the representations of the senses are not *repraesentationes sentenciae*, but are given.",[]],"busolt-s19-009":["A *conceptus* can also be only *intellectualis*. From just this arises the logical form. Such a *conceptus* is called *notio, et oppositum huius est conceptus sensitiuus, seu potius empiricus* [notion, and the opposite of this is the sensitive, or rather empirical, concept]. These *notiones* can be presented *in concreto*. But concepts *a priori*, of which no object can be given *in concreto*, are ideas go beyond experience, but are nevertheless no chimeras.",[]],"busolt-s19-010":["The *conceptus* were divided into *conceptus dati* [given concepts] and *factittii seu arbitrarii* [made or arbitrary] *Conceptus dati* are the concepts, which are given to me of a thing, *conceptus factitii*, those which I have actually made for myself E.g. Every inventor of a work of art has of the works to be produced *conceptum arbitrarium seu factitium* *Conceptus dati sunt dati vel per experientiam* [given concepts are given either through experience] as *conceptus coloris rub ri* [the concept of the color red] *vel per naturam intellectus* [or through the nature of the understanding], as: the concept of substance. Representations are either *a priori* or empirical, we have",[]],"busolt-s19-011":["a.) *Conceptus factitios a priori*.",[]],"busolt-s19-012":["b.) *Conceptus factitios a posteriori*, mathematics occupies itself with *conceptibus factitiis*. The *conceptus dati* are either *a priori* or *a posteriori*. These are empirical those are pure. E.g. All concepts of colors, of taste are *conceptus dati a posteriori* of virtue *a priori*.",[]],"busolt-s19-013":["The question of logic is, how representations become concepts? They therefore consider the form. How does a concept come from intuition? to this belongs",[]],"busolt-s19-014":["a.) Reflection, or attention to the manifold which is thought in the intuition.",[]],"busolt-s19-015":["b.) Comparation, or the comparison of diversity and of identity, e.g. The comparison between spruces and firs, and the willows; those have needles and these leaves; I therefore have a concept of needle-wood and leaf-wood these two pieces belong to the positive; to the negative belongs",[]],"busolt-s19-016":["c.) *Abstractio*, since I forget all diversities and consider merely the identity.",[]],"busolt-s19-017":["A concept is that which is contained in many things, the *Repraesentatio communis* [common representation]. *Repraesentatio communis est quae efficit identitatem* [a common representation is that which effects the identity] of the manifold. In the *Intuitu* I can have more representations, *in conceptu* only those, which are common to different things. *Usus conceptuum est vel in abstracto, vel in concreto, non vero conceptus ipse.* [The use of concepts is either *in abstracto* or *in concreto*, but not the concept itself.] *In abstracto*, when I separate a concept from all manifoldness e.g. The concept of human beings in general.",[]],"busolt-s19-018":["These are of great utility, and must precede the other: For how can I e.g. make a concept of a king, if I do not first make a concept of the human being. Although one makes the reproach to abstract concepts that they are not intelligible and therefore unfruitful, one answers to this: one takes from them only a mark common to many things, by which one can set out much.",[]],"busolt-s19-019":["From the concept *in abstracto* I must gradually descend to the concept *in concreto*, because otherwise there is no application. Concepts are divided into high and low. The matter is that which I represent in the concept. It can therefore have more and also less representation. The domain consists in that which stands under the concept, and which pertains to all the manifold. This comprehends a certain magnitude of the concept, or of the domain. A concept can therefore have a greater sphere, a greater application, than another, the more I think a concept *in abstracto*, the greater becomes the sphere, and the content decreases. The more I think a concept *in concreto* the smaller becomes its sphere but it grows in content. *Conceptus singularis* has no sphere at all. A concept is called *Superior* only in consideration, and comparison of those standing under it; the one standing under it is called *inferior*. The difference is therefore relative. A *Conceptus* must have a sphere, *conceptus* which comprehends the others in itself is called *genus*, that which is contained in others is called *species*. E.g. Iron is the *Species* of metal. A *Genus* considered in another can also be *Species*. E.g. Metal is *Species* of body. Further a *Species* can also be *genus*. E.g. Iron is the *Genus* of bar iron. The difference consists in the relation; and so we come always from the *Genus* to the *Species* in order finally to come to the *indiuiduum*. The greater the concept *in abstracto* is, the greater is the sphere. The *Conceptus summus* [highest concept] which has the greatest sphere, has the greatest *Genus*, and from this first *genere* we come *gradatim* [by degrees] to the *indiuiduum*, which one could call *conceptum singularem*. We can compare through the sphere only when the concepts are of the same kind. The difference of the *Specierum* which belong under the same *Genus*, is *differentia specifica*. *Differentia numerica est, si res specie eadem sunt numero diuerso*: [It is numerical difference, if things the same in species are diverse in number:] *Conceptus superior* is called *Latior* [wider]; *inferior* is called *angustior* [narrower]. But if the sphere of the *conceptuum* is equal i.e. if the difference of the *conceptuum* is infinitely small, then these are *conceptus reciproci*. The author speaks of the *conceptu universali et particulari*, but only the use of the *Conceptuum* is either universal or particular. Perhaps he understands by the *conceptui universali*, *conceptum communem*; but this is every concept.",[]],"busolt-s20-001":["Definitions are distinctly determined concepts they are:",[]],"busolt-s20-002":["a.) Analytic and",[]],"busolt-s20-003":["b.) Synthetic.",[]],"busolt-s20-004":["The analytic is the definition of a given concept, in which the manifold is made distinct through dissection, the synthetic is the definition of a made concept i.e. the arbitrary composition of the manifold by which I make a concept of the thing distinct To analysis belongs",[]],"busolt-s20-005":["1.) A concept must be given.",[]],"busolt-s20-006":["2.) I must add nothing to this, but make it distinct to myself through analysis. According to synthesis I can say I will e.g. that a tetrahedron be a body, composed of 4 equilateral surfaces.",[]],"busolt-s20-007":["Synthesis can be *a priori* also *posteriori*. In regard to all made concepts a definition must be given. Mathematics begins at all times with definitions, for it has to do merely with made concepts. it is a pure synthesis of magnitude. To be sure it also treats of space, but because it is not a made concept, it also does not define it, but takes it as known to everyone. A synthetic definition of a thing cannot be false: For I thereby make the *definitum*, i.e. the concept, which I have of the thing through the definition.",[]],"busolt-s20-008":["A given concept is either given *a priori*. E.g. The concept of virtue, of space etc. etc. or *a posteriori* e.g. the concept of metal. Both are defined through analysis, e.g. *a priori*, if I am to define the cause, then I dissect the concept of cause and find, that to it belongs;",[]],"busolt-s20-009":["a.) A thing which happens,",[]],"busolt-s20-010":["b.) A thing from which it follows, and then I define e.g. *a posteriori*, if I define the body, then I analyze its properties, and give a perfect definition. The object of the empirical concept one cannot explain for everyone already has a perfect concept of it. E.g. what earth is, what water etc. It does not go through *analysis*, because it is in vain to dissect that which can be made distinct through the explanation. Nor through synthesis: for I can only describe and collect concepts. We have empirically given and also empirically made concepts. Empirically given concepts can and should not be defined: for the marks are not sufficient to give the definition an exhaustive distinctness, they are perhaps only infinitely small parts of it, but description does take place with them. E.g. Gold I cannot define; but describe. With the empirically made concepts the *data* are given to us from experience, and from just these we form our concept. These are found where nature is altered e.g. the concept of a house, of a fortress.",[]],"busolt-s20-011":["All definition lays out the content of the concept, the analytic as well as synthetic the difference between definition and description consists in this; that the first makes the concept completely distinct the other on the other hand only extends it. It has to do with the content, insofar as we have it. With empirical made concepts I declare, think, what I want to say. This happens through experience, not *a priori* Concepts *a priori* contain a definition. In natural science descriptions take place, and if I think several things in the same thing; then this requires a declaration.",[]],"busolt-s21-001":["This happens through analysis. Here the difficulty is, whether the analysis is also complete? This is very rare. Notwithstanding this many correct *Konsectarien* [corollaries] can be derived from these imperfections I can indeed know that I know nothing more; but that nothing is lacking no one can know. Thus an analytic definition can never attain to apodictic certainty. From the mathematician I can demand definition: for through the definition he makes the concept. But he can also give it.",[]],"busolt-s21-002":["From the philosopher on the other hand I cannot demand it. For he cannot determine whether the analysis is also complete. But although he cannot have a complete analysis, he can still have an incomplete one, which gives him some marks. As long as such an analysis is incomplete, it is not called definition. The materials of the definition are called elementary propositions i.e. incomplete propositions for a definition. *Definitio est conceptus, qui rem adaequat.* [A definition is a concept which is adequate to the thing.] If a concept has distinct, but too few marks, then it is incomplete, if it has too many marks, then it is not precise. A concept which has not too few not too many marks, i.e. which is adequate to the thing, *conceptus qui adaequat rem, est definitio*. A definition can therefore have *completudinem* [completeness], but if it does not have precision, then it is still faulty for something superfluous, not something neat precise is stated. Opposed to precision is tautology, where the same mark occurs twice, although particular times hidden. E.g. God is an infinite, eternal, omniscient being. From the concept infinite all other properties already flow. With made concepts the definition goes first, with given ones I first make the concept distinct, I analyze it, and then I give a definition.",[]],"busolt-s21-003":["A definition must be complete; but I can, if not complete, yet have incomplete distinct concepts, and then an *exposition* of the elementary concepts is required. Of concepts *a priori* a definition is indeed possible, but not necessary, but *expositio* is. With concepts *a posteriori* there can be not definition but description. A distinction is to be made between a nominal and real definition Nominal definition is where marks are stated, in order to have insight into the identity and diversity. This would be an outer use of the definition. Real definition or also genetic definition, when marks are stated in order to distinguish the *Object* from all others. This would be an inner use of the definition. The nominal and real definition given by Wolff is here censured. If a definition is to contain the difference from all possible concepts, then the marks must be entirely complete. It must therefore be nominal and also real definition. Nominal definitions are therefore not needed unless a misunderstanding of the expression is feared. Only with Latin terms is that needed. These terms must be adequate to the thing and its genesis. Etymology thus furnishes particular explanations of names. Explanations of names serve merely to distinguish objects from others.",[]],"busolt-s21-004":["Description holds merely of the empirical. Definitions which cannot be defined, insofar as they are to be objectively sufficient, are themselves an incomplete *exposition* and always serve also as definition *ad instantem vsum* [for the present use]. In intellectual things it is admittedly not possible, yet it can be nominal here too, and besides that be incomplete *exposition*. For it is still always approximation to definition. For an incomplete concept *et hoc sensu definitione procedere potest descriptio* [and in this sense description can proceed from (or: precede) definition].",[]],"busolt-s21-005":["Remark: One can best distinguish whether one has not described something, if one passes backwards from experience to reason, and can explain genetically *a priori* that which one previously had to demonstrate *a posteriori*. E.g. Thus one could easily give a definition *a priori* of the lunar eclipses as soon as one knows that the moon receives its light from the sun, and the earth sometimes comes between both. Chemistry composes, and that can then be defined. If I can accordingly resolve the object into the *notas* which make up what is essential to it then that is called primary materials. With the definition one must ask, whether the definition as a proposition is true. E.g. A human being is a human being, this is certainly true as a proposition. The soul is a thinking being. In definition one finds the greatest disunity of philosophy. E.g. virtue is a certain facility in certain actions. This is not correct. For",[]],"busolt-s21-006":["a.) Virtue is a free action",[]],"busolt-s21-007":["b.) A firm resolution",[]],"busolt-s21-008":["c.) A persisting disposition in the exercise of right.",[]],"busolt-s21-009":["Of the nature of things I can say much through analysis. Analytic propositions are certainly true, but sometimes a great tautology can be stuck in them. This is the second thing that must be investigated.",[]],"busolt-s21-010":["3.) Whether the concept is also complete. This *Completudo* arises from the confluence of all marks. Time is the order of things, insofar as they follow one another.",[]],"busolt-s21-011":["4.) Whether the concept is also precise, i.e. that no more marks are added than are needed. The superfluous marks cannot be *compartes* [co-parts] of the others.",[]],"busolt-s21-012":["The cause of the lack of precision is that I misplace a mark To say something *Definite* means to frame the concept so that nothing more, nothing less is said than the concept contains. To definition therefore belongs:",[]],"busolt-s21-013":["Truth, *completudo* and distinctness. In definition I therefore proceed:",[]],"busolt-s21-014":["a.) I analyze my concept and seek true propositions.",[]],"busolt-s21-015":["b.) The propositions must contain marks, by which the concept of the *definiti* becomes distinct.",[]],"busolt-s21-016":["c.) I collect as many marks as are necessary to bring out a complete concept",[]],"busolt-s21-017":["A definition of description we cannot give, because it belongs to philosophy, and in this the first is a procedure where one defines, the other a procedure where one describes.",[]],"busolt-s21-018":["When we try to explain much in nature, we can see from this whether one has understood the explanation; if one pays heed whether the proposition could also be cognized *a priori*, from the very same grounds. The lunar eclipse one can also explain *a priori*, if one knows that the earth steps between the sun and the moon. Further if one sees whether one could also make the concept E.g. The definition of cinnabar, quicksilver and sulfur sublimated gives cinnabar: I can therefore make and define cinnabar. *Definitio nec sit latior nec sit angustior; seu nec plus, nec minus suo in se continet definito.* [Let the definition be neither wider nor narrower; or it contains in itself neither more nor less than its *definitum*.] *Definitio ne sit per circulum* [let the definition not be through a circle] means, I want to explain a thing through itself, I bring the *definiendum* into the definition. One has a *circulum vitiosum in demonstrando* [vicious circle in demonstrating], when I lay at the ground the proposition which I want to prove. One also has *definitum obscurum in definiendo*, when I want to use another concept, which is also obscure, for the distinctness of the other. *Definitio ne sit Tautologica.* [Let the definition not be tautological.] E.g. The soul is a rational being, which can judge here the faculty of judging already lies under the rational being. This tautology can be *explicite* also *implicite*.",[]],"busolt-s22-001":["This is distinguished from dissection. In logical division I look to the sphere i.e. to the extensive application of a concept. Logical division is therefore the division of the sphere. The greater the sphere therefore is, the more it can be divided. Logical division contains the difference of various concepts, insofar as they are contained under a common concept. If all disjunctive concepts taken together are equal to the *Sphaera conceptus*, then this is a logical perfect division e.g. triangles are either equilateral",[]],"busolt-s22-002":["or isosceles. The other concepts are called *membra dividentia* [dividing members] must be equal to the *Sphaera conceptus divisi* [sphere of the divided concept] there is a codivision also subdivision of a *membri*. The subdivision is the division of a *Membri dividentis* E.g. All human beings are learned or unlearned. Codivisions are therefore divisions where I divide the concept under different sections. They are side-divisions where namely I consider the concept under another sphere. The errors in logical divisions are:",[]],"busolt-s22-003":["a.) If the *Membra dividentia* are not opposed to one another",[]],"busolt-s22-004":["b). The members of the subdivision must not be brought under the members of the division e.g. Triangles are equilateral or isosceles. This division is false. One would have to divide them thus. Triangles are either equal in all 3 sides, or unequal. The unequal are either isosceles, which have two equal sides, or scalene, where no side is equal. The easiest division is dichotomy. All substances are either bodies; or not; this is the foundation of all logical definitions.",[]],"busolt-s23-001":["We cognize something immediately, or mediately, i.e. we cognize our cognitions of the object, such a mediate cognition of the cognition of the object is a judgment I can therefore immediately through a concept only represent something, or I can have a concept through mediate cognition. I can therefore pass a judgment directly of the object and through this I can again pass a judgment of the cognition of the object. The relation of one cognition to another insofar as the one is the means of the other, is the judgment.",[]],"busolt-s24-001":["All judgments are brought under four titles, and divided according to four *Functionen*, namely;",[]],"busolt-s24-002":["a.) Quantity",[]],"busolt-s24-003":["b.) Quality.",[]],"busolt-s24-004":["c.) Relation.",[]],"busolt-s24-005":["d.) Modality.",[]],"busolt-s24-006":["Each of these has three *modos*. Judgments according to quality are either affirmative, or negative or infinite judgments. According to quantity, are either universal, or particular, or singular. According to relation, are either categorical, or hypothetical, or disjunctive. And according to modality, either problematic, or assertoric, or apodictic. E.g. All human beings are mortal. This judgment is according to quality affirmative, according to quantity universal, according to relation categorical, according to modality assertoric. The judgments of the two first titles one distinguishes well.",[]],"busolt-s24-007":["1.) But how does one distinguish the judgment of relation? We have three kinds of relations",[]],"busolt-s24-008":["a.) The *Subiect* to the predicate, i.e. categorical;",[]],"busolt-s24-009":["b.) The ground to the sequence, i.e. hypothetical",[]],"busolt-s24-010":["c.) The relation of the members among one another, and to the divided concept, i.e. disjunctive, the relation of the members among one another is when they are opposed. All human beings are either virtuous or vicious.",[]],"busolt-s24-011":["2.) The judgments of modality.",[]],"busolt-s24-012":["If I hold a judgment to be possible, take it merely as a touchstone, of what might well follow from it then this is a problematic judgment. A judgment with consciousness of truth is assertoric. A judgment which is necessarily true is apodictic. E.g. If I say the moon could well have inhabitants, then that is problematic, if I say it has inhabitants; then this is assertoric, and if I wanted to say it must have inhabitants, then that is judged apodictically. With problematic judgments therefore the sequence remains clear, but the ground, or the one antecedent proposition is undecided. A judgment is therefore a cognition of a cognition, or a mediate cognition of the object: for every concept is merely a cognition. E.g. Living and creatures are concepts, but human beings are living creatures, is a judgment. In the definition of judgment I cannot weigh the concept alone: for there are judgments where only the relation of two concepts is needed, that is categorical; but with others the relation of two, or more judgments is also needed i.e. with the hypothetical and disjunctive. The first definition, that a judgment is a cognition of a cognition, thus remains indeed the best. That the definition of judgment is not so evident comes from this because we already judge when we define.",[]],"busolt-s24-013":["Remark All actions of the understanding go to judgments and every object that we cognize, every concept is as it were a predicate to a possible judgment; so one can explain the understanding which one previously explained as the faculty of concepts, as the faculty of judgments, or rules: For the understanding is the source of rules, because every judgment is a rule every rule a judgment; e.g. All human beings are mortal. As soon as I therefore see a human being, this proposition is as it were a rule to me, this one too must die. Just this definition of the understanding agrees with the definition which Wolff gave, namely that the understanding is the faculty of distinct representations: For only through judgments does the logical distinctness of representations arise. Accordingly distinct representations ought to be spoken of with judgments because a distinct concept already presupposes a judgment. A distinct concept is namely possible through a clear mark but the representation is just as much as a predicate of a judgment. The representation of the object itself is the *Subiect*, consequently I have *Materiam sc. dati iudicii* [the matter, namely, of a given judgment]. The concept a human being is clear, a rational human being, distinct: For here I represent the human being by means of the predicate rational, and thus have a judgment. Distinctness is twofold.",[]],"busolt-s24-014":["a.) In the concept, this is the logical *haec respicit intellectum* [this regards the understanding].",[]],"busolt-s24-015":["b.) In intuition, this is the aesthetic *sententia haec respicit sens us* [this regards the senses].",[]],"busolt-s24-016":["We can have aesthetic distinctness of things and the logical can be lacking to us. E.g. A servant stands in a palace so he has of it indeed a sensitive distinctness, but since he does not know what cornices, what columns are, the logical distinctness is lacking to him. Logical distinctness belongs properly only to the philosopher. In every judgment there occur matter and form.",[]],"busolt-s24-017":["a.) Matter i.e. the cognition by means of which I cognize an *Object*.",[]],"busolt-s24-018":["b.) The form, i.e. the relation of the cognitions. E.g. The human being is mortal; here human being and mortal are the matter, but the relation of the human being to mortality is the form.",[]],"busolt-s24-019":["Because logic abstracts from all matter; there remains to it merely the form, and this is considered in a fourfold respect, as just adduced. The essential form of judgments consists in relation, for concepts have sphere, quality modality, but relation is proper to the *Judicio*. Categorical judgments lie at the ground of the others: For the hypothetical consist of two categorical judgments, of which I consider one as the ground, the other as the sequence. The disjunctive consist of several categorical judgments. The disjunctive taken together are equal to the *conceptu diviso*.",[]],"busolt-s24-020":["Because the *Judicia kategorica* are thus the *data iudiciorum hypotheticorum et disiunctorum* [data of hypothetical and disjunctive judgments]; the logicians regard the first as the only true judgments and said that the others could be reduced to them. When the author speaks of *subiect*, *praedicat* and *copula*; this is understood only of categorical judgments: for with the hypothetical *antecedens* and *consequens* are to be distinguished, and with the disjunctive *membra diuidentia* and *conceptus divisi*. We will therefore speak.",[]],"busolt-s25-001":["These contain two concepts of things, and with them there is distinguished",[]],"busolt-s25-002":["a.) *Subiect* is the concept of which the other is a mark, or *est conceptus, quem per alium cognitio* [is the concept which (I cognize) through another].",[]],"busolt-s25-003":["b.) *Praedicat* is a concept which is a mark of the other, or *est conceptus per quem subiectum repraesento cognitione* [is the concept through which I represent the subject (in) cognition].",[]],"busolt-s25-004":["c.) The *Copula* is that by which the relation of the *conceptuum* is designated.",[]],"busolt-s25-005":["Categorical judgments are divided, *quoad qualitatem* [as to quality] into *affirmatiua negatiua* and *infinita*",[]],"busolt-s25-006":["a.) *Judicium affirmatiuum*, when the *Copula est* expresses the combination of the *Subiect* and *Praedicat*.",[]],"busolt-s25-007":["b.) *Judicium negatiuum*, when the copula *non est* is the *opposition* of the subject and predicate, or the negation of the judgment is thus expressed through the negation of the relation, the *negation* must therefore stand with the copula and not with the predicate: For if the latter happens, then the concept is indeed negative but not the judgment: for logic looks in judgments merely to the relation",[]],"busolt-s25-008":["*Judicium infinitum* when the predicate is negated, *quoad quantitatem* (as to quantity) into *universalia*, *particularia*, and *singularia*.",[]],"busolt-s25-009":["1.) *Judicium universale*, where the predicate is predicated of the whole *sphaera*.",[]],"busolt-s25-010":["2.) *Judicium particulare*, where the predicate is predicated of a part of the *sphaera*.",[]],"busolt-s25-011":["3.) *Judicium singulare*, where the subject lacks a *sphaera* and therefore the predicate is predicated of the whole subject.",[]],"busolt-s25-012":["The *Judicia singularia* can be considered as *Judicia vniversalia*, insofar as the predicate holds of the subject without exception, and therefore the *Judicia singularia* are also left out by most, with right. One also speaks of *generalia* and *Specialia*, but *generalia* are not *universalia*, and *Specialia* are not *particularia*. E.g. To lecture on geography *generaliter* does not mean to lecture on it *vniversaliter*. For geography holds only *particulariter* of the earth *Generalia* are those that pertain to the genus, and *specialia*, those that pertain to the species. Some judgments are so constituted that one must limit the concept of the subject in order that the judgment be valid. E.g. All angles in a triangle together make up 180 degrees but this holds only of the rectilinear and not of the curvilinear triangles Likewise all bodies are heavy, but now if no other body is there according to which it can direct itself, how can it be heavy? Therefore this concept too must be limited in relation to another body In such judgments, therefore, the subject or better the universality of the subject must be limited, in order that this predicate belong to it. Such judgments now, where the *assertion* is conditioned, are called conditioned judgments. But if the condition is posited not to the subject; but to the copula, then the judgment is called *Judicium modale*, e.g. All bodies are contingently heavy; from this then arises the so-called *modalitas iudiciorum*, wherein occur: possibility actuality, necessity. Remark This modality is most important: for how often are possibility and necessity not confused with one another. E.g. In the demonstration of the immortality of the soul. In order to express the judgments of quality and quantity, letters have been chosen, which here is probably of no use, except only in syllogistics, namely: *Aßerit A, negat E, sed vniversaliter ambo Aßerit I, negat O, sed particulariter ambo.*",[]],"busolt-s25-013":["The logicians express modality by the question *Quae propositio?* Quality by *Qualis est propositio?* Quantity by *Quanta est propositio.*",[]],"busolt-s25-014":["Up to here we have spoken of simple judgments, but there are also composite judgments. E.g. God, the soul and all finite",[]],"busolt-s25-015":["spirits are immortal. Composite judgments rest on this, that either",[]],"busolt-s25-016":["a.) subject or",[]],"busolt-s25-017":["b.) predicate judgments are.",[]],"busolt-s26-001":["Hypothetical judgments consist of two judgments that have a relation of the *Consequens*, and to this belongs",[]],"busolt-s26-002":["a. *Antecedens*, or that which precedes the sequence, That is the ground.",[]],"busolt-s26-003":["b. *Consequens*, or that which follows from the *Antecedens*, that is the sequence. They therefore have a relation of the *nexus* of judgments. Disjunctive judgments consist of two, or several judgments, which have a relation of *opposition*, which *opposition*, however, must be contradictory, the *membra* must therefore be contradictory, but together be equal to the *Conceptui diuerso*.",[]],"busolt-s26-004":["An exponible judgment is one which is at the same time affirmative and negative. For such judgments the words \"alone,\" \"only few\" etc. etc. serve in German e.g. Few human beings are pious, some human beings are pious, some are not pious Aesthetic judgments we distinguish from logical ones by this, when besides the concepts there is also a sensation e.g. O! How many human beings are godless! This is an aesthetic judgment. If we take a proposition as practical, then we can say: it is *formaliter* practical and then it is opposed to the theoretical, or it is *materialiter* practical, then it is opposed to the speculative. As to form, practical propositions are those according to which an exercise is possible, here it is not the relation of cognitions but the relation of freedom. These practical propositions stand opposed to the theoretical. E.g. A triangle consists of three straight lines, this is theoretical. To describe a triangle from three given straight lines, is formally practical. Of such practical propositions, as are opposed to the speculative, logic knows nothing.",[]],"busolt-s26-005":["A cognition theoretical as to form, from which many propositions can be derived that I ought to do, or which contains many imperatives, is practical as to matter. E.g. There is a single judge of the world. But logic, which abstracts from all content, knows nothing of this, that some cognitions are so fruitful. The practical propositions in logic must be, to make an inference from given judgments, and a *Terminus medius*. The author speaks of provable and unprovable judgments. Provable judgments are those which a judgment must precede, or which for the confirmation of their truth require a proof. Unprovable judgments are those which need no proof at all, but are already true in themselves. The first are mediately certain the others immediately certain judgments. Often we say a proposition is immediately certain, although it is not. Hence one must first take a judgment to be mediately certain; because we",[]],"busolt-s26-006":["a.) Perhaps also sense the result of the proof in us but do not have the proof itself.",[]],"busolt-s26-007":["b.) Because the human understanding is more inclined to take something as mediately certain. For it always wants to go further, we must therefore deal carefully with these propositions, and first examine them precisely.",[]],"busolt-s26-008":["The author calls an unfathomable cognition empty, because through it our cognition increases by nothing, and also loses nothing. But a cognition can be",[]],"busolt-s26-009":["a.) empty of sense i.e. a proposition that is empty as to matter.",[]],"busolt-s26-010":["b.) Empty as to the form of the understanding, e.g. The human being has a human essence.",[]],"busolt-s26-011":["These propositions that explain *idem per idem* advance cognition neither analytically, nor synthetically, they are tautological propositions, through them I have neither an increase in distinctness, nor a growth in cognition. But not all identical propositions are empty propositions; but only the tautological ones. An identical proposition that can be seen into only through *analysis* is not to be regarded as an empty proposition: For it helps toward distinctness. As to content a proposition is empty, if its representations refer to no subject. The identity in tautology is cognized immediately, but genuine identity mediately, i.e. through the taking apart of concepts. These tautological propositions help and harm nothing. But we perceive even the true as absurd, if it is conducive to none of our aims. Negative propositions do not indeed extend our cognitions, but they remedy errors, whereas false propositions harm. E.g. the soul is not composite. If I use something negatively that no human being has ever called into doubt then it becomes absurd.",[]],"busolt-s26-012":["*Propositiones indemonstrabiles* are hypothetical propositions which have arisen not through dissection of the subject, but from elsewhere, synthetic propositions *a priori*, if they are intuitive *a priori*, are *axiomata*. Philosophy does not have false judgments *a priori*, but insofar as it is discursive philosophy does indeed have *Principia axiomata*. Axiom means as much as a dignity that is distinct in itself, and need not be proved, it already brings a certainty with it. The author treats *Judicia intuitiua* and *discursiua*.",[]],"busolt-s26-013":["a.) *Judicia intuitiua* are, when they represent something concrete *in singulari*.",[]],"busolt-s26-014":["These are either",[]],"busolt-s26-015":["1.) *A priori* intuitive i.e. pure intuiting concepts (*iudicia*) mathematical.",[]],"busolt-s26-016":["2.) *A posteriori* empirically intuiting *iudicia*.",[]],"busolt-s26-017":["*Consectarium* is a proposition of consequence, especially in philosophy. *Corollarium* is a proposition, from which the other is concluded through an inference, especially in mathematics. *Corollarium est ratio cinatio monosyllogistica* (a corollary is a monosyllogistic ratiocination). A theorem is a theoretical proposition, insofar as it is capable of and in need of a proof It is nothing other, than an *est ratio cinatio polysyllogistica*, i.e. a proposition that is concluded from other preceding inferences. The requisites of the theorem are.",[]],"busolt-s26-018":["a. Proposition",[]],"busolt-s26-019":["b. Demonstration.",[]],"busolt-s26-020":["*Problema* is a practical proposition different from the previous one. For the requisites of the problem are:",[]],"busolt-s26-021":["a.) *Quaestio Problematis*, through which it is set out what ought to happen.",[]],"busolt-s26-022":["b.) *Resolutio quaestionis*, in what way it ought to happen.",[]],"busolt-s26-023":["c.) *Demonstratio*, that this *resolutio* is true",[]],"busolt-s26-024":["These problems belong especially in mathematics yet there are also particular ones, as in morals, medicine, of the human soul.",[]],"busolt-s26-025":["It is thus a very important rule *ne sit tautologica problematis resolutio, i.e. ne resolutionem praedicamus in definitionem* (let the resolution of the problem not be tautological, i.e. let us not predicate the resolution in the definition). In morals there are thus, as said, too many such problematic resolutions it comes precisely from this, that morals as canon is already actually there, i.e. that we actually have rules, according to which we steer the judgments of the understanding, and estimations of moral actions, but not as organon, i.e. morals has not yet been expounded so that the concepts of the understanding flow into my will and inclination, that virtue is not only approved but also practiced. In the tautological *resolution* of the problem one analyzes the definition itself, in morals the treatment need not be exactly *praecise*, but in mathematics no *abundance* can take place for although otherwise precision is an essential property of a system; yet some sciences are excepted from it, and to these belong besides morals also the physical sciences",[]],"busolt-s26-026":["1. Definitions, axioms, corollaries, theorems with their demonstrations, problems also with resolutions and demonstrations, these are the parts of a system, and of a dogmatic one.",[]],"busolt-s26-027":["But to such a dogmatic system belong:",[]],"busolt-s26-028":["a.) *Partes domesticae*, which properly constitute the system.",[]],"busolt-s26-029":["b.) *Partes peregrinae*, which are drawn as it were from another field, as in moral philosophy on the existence of God, so in metaphysics examples are taken from physics; a part of this kind is called *lemma*, which is a foreign part of the system, worked out from another science.",[]],"busolt-s26-030":["2.) *Partes essentiales et extra essentiales.*",[]],"busolt-s26-031":["a.) *Partes essentiales* are those which being removed, the whole system is removed.",[]],"busolt-s26-032":["b.) *Partes extra essentiales*, by which this is illustrated, the part alien to the system is not extended; such is the *Notion*. That is properly the resting place of the system, in it one mentions the history the use of the system, in it one refutes the opponents. They are for the most part aesthetic.",[]],"busolt-s26-033":["A *Qualitaet nulla* is so called in logic where the profession of ignorance of the inquirer is taken for the definition of the thing itself and the discovery of the cause, e.g. what then is the cause of the propagation of animals and plants? answer: *vis plastica*. Here I define *idem per idem*, and it is as much said as: The cause of this effect is hidden. What does a cognition now gain from such an an swer?",[]],"busolt-s26-034":["Remark *Qualitas occulta* is a tautological explanation of phenomena and indeed regards the subject, not the object. Of this kind is Lambert's *ideosyncrasia*, *cuius ait hominis sibi propinquam habitum cane et angue aversantem. Et vero ideosyncrasia quali, quae in hoc illudere indiuiduum influit.* Of just this kind is the word *bon sens* (healthy understanding), so called in recent times. For who has given a definition of *Sensu communi*, unless tautological? even the Crusian philosophy rests on a principle that is nothing other than *Qualitas occulta*, namely what I cannot find otherwise than true, that is true. The Newtonian philosophy, which is grounded on the principle of the reciprocal sphere in the interconnection of the course of the world, takes the word *vis attractiva* for *qualitatem occultam* in the physical sense, that is merely as a confession of ignorance of the true causes, but not for the true cause itself.",[]],"busolt-s26-035":["If a judgment is concluded from another, without an intermediate judgment, then this is an inference of the understanding, *per iudicium intermedium* an inference of reason, e.g. All human beings are mortal, therefore Caius too is mortal. Here now Caius is a concept, which I did not have before, it is therefore falsely inferred, and I must only infer mediately, by making for myself a concept of Caius. For *consequentia immediata* *iudicium praeuium* and *exclusio* must receive a concept. They are not otherwise distinguished than only in quality, quantity and relation. The *Consequentiae immediatae* occur",[]],"busolt-s26-036":["a.) *Per iudicia aequipollentia*",[]],"busolt-s26-037":["b.) *Per iudicia subalternata.*",[]],"busolt-s26-038":["c.) *Per iudicia opposita.*",[]],"busolt-s26-039":["d.) *Per conversionem iudiciorum*",[]],"busolt-s26-040":["e.) *Per contrapositionem iudiciorum.*",[]],"busolt-s26-041":["The first kind can drop out, it is empty, because it is as good as nothing, to conclude one from the other, what is the same as the former, it always remains the same judgment. In the second way I infer from the universal to the particular. In the third way one is to infer immediately *per iudicia opposita*. This *opposition* is threefold.",[]],"busolt-s26-042":["a.) Contradictory, here of two judgments only one can be true, and I thus infer: if one is true; then the other is false and conversely. For this it is required that one be universally affirmative the other particularly negative, or that one be universally negative, the other particularly affirmative. E.g. All human beings are mortal, some are not mortal. No substance is simple, some are simple:",[]],"busolt-s26-043":["b.) Subcontrary, where one judgment is particularly affirmative the other particularly negative e.g. Some human beings are evil, – some are not evil.",[]],"busolt-s26-044":["c.) Contrary, when to a universal affirmative judgment a universally negative judgment is opposed, or conversely, e.g. All human beings are vicious. At such a proposition the other can be so horrified, that he says: No human being is vicious. Here he does more than is necessary, to contradict the other *Judicia contraria opposita* can both be false, because in them more is negated than is necessary. The falsity thus lies in the universality. In the following kinds one changes subject and predicate, but in *conversion* one can change the quantity, but not the quality. One calls the transposition of the terms *metathesis terminorum*. Through this there arises an immediate inference through the *conversion* of judgments, where the quantity is transposed and not the quality, and through the *contraposition* of judgments, where the quality is transposed and not the quantity. E.g. All human beings are mortal some mortals are therefore human beings, i.e. through conversion What is not mortal, is no human being i.e. through *contraposition*. In the *conversion* of judgments the judgment that is to be converted is called: *Judicium conversum*, that through which the conversion arises *iudicium convertens*. The conversion occurs either *per accidens* or *simpliciter*.",[]],"busolt-s26-045":["The conditions as to form are these;",[]],"busolt-s26-046":["a.) Universal affirmative propositions can be converted only *per accidens*, universally negative propositions only *simpliciter* and thirdly particularly affirmative or negative propositions also *simpliciter*. The first means as much as *iudicium conuersum est vniversale* and of these I can now say conversely some mortals are human beings I think namely of mortals among whom are also human beings, i.e. the subject human being is under the sphere of mortals, as of the predicate. The predicate must thus be greater than the subject, I can thus take some mortals for human beings. There are universal affirmative propositions that can be converted *simpliciter*, in which the sphere of the subject and predicate is equal e.g. All figures enclosed in three straight lines, are triangles, all triangles are figures enclosed in three straight lines are triangles, i.e. a subtlety and I leave it here to each, how many out of an infinite multitude of triangles he wants to except. But this distinction is *euanescens*. All negative judgments, which contain a conflict, can be converted *simpliciter*, e.g. no triangle is without angles no figure without angles is a triangle all particular affirmative or negative propositions can be converted *simpliciter*, i.e. that the quantity is not altered. Contraposition means as much as: every universal proposition can be contraposed. E.g. All human beings are mortal, non-mortals are not human beings. How is one to infer according to the *Judiciis mediatis*? If one concept is to be inferred from the other, which is not contained in the previous one; then one must add a concept that contains the condition of the rules; the concept of the object now contains the condition of the rule, and thus of this *conclusion* the predicate of the conclusion must also hold. E.g. All human beings are mortal, Caius is a human being, therefore Caius is mortal. This inference of reason is grounded on the principle: to every thing insofar as it can be subsumed under the condition the predicate belongs but an inference of reason is *cognitio a priori per subsumtionem conceptus sub conditione regulae cuiusdam generali*.",[]],"busolt-s26-047":["The inference of reason has two requisitions",[]],"busolt-s26-048":["a.) Universal rule i.e. *Maior*.",[]],"busolt-s26-049":["b.) The subsumption of a concept under the condition of this universal rule i.e. *Minor*.",[]],"busolt-s26-050":["c.) The combination of the predicate with the subject, or that which belongs from the universal rule of the subsumption, this is the conclusion. *Maior* and *Minor* are called premises. The premises and the conclusion is thus that which belongs to the inference.",[]],"busolt-s26-051":["In these inferences one looks to the relation, and according to this the inferences are divided into categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive. All inferences are judged according to the *Maiore propositione*. If that is a categorical judgment then the inference is also categorical. Only in categorical inferences is there subject predicate and *Terminus medius*, and therefore one easily sees the importance, that it was said: for an inference of reason 3 *Termini*: *Maior* or the predicate of the conclusion, *minor* or the subject of the conclusion and *Terminus medius*, which is the condition of the conclusion, are required; every inference of reason consists in general of 3 pieces.",[]],"busolt-s26-052":["a.) Premises.",[]],"busolt-s26-053":["b.) *Maior* and *Minor* and",[]],"busolt-s26-054":["c.) Conclusion.",[]],"busolt-s26-055":["The matter for inferences of reason are the premises or propositions from which the conclusion follows. The form consists in the consequence of the conclusion from the premises. An inference of reason can be correct in matter, but fail in form. Only in categorical inferences is there a *Medius Terminus*. In the hypothetical there is merely the relation of two kinds of judgments, how one follows from the correctness of the other.",[]],"busolt-s27-001":["To the chief rule of inferences of reason others otherwise count the proposition of contradiction. No judgment is true, where the predicate contradicts the subject. But this *Principium* of *Contradiction* cannot be used as an inference of reason: for it is only a *Principium iudicii*.",[]],"busolt-s27-002":["An inference of reason is affirmative, if the conclusion is affirmative, and negative, if the conclusion is negative. According to this universal division of all inferences of reason we make 2. particular rules:",[]],"busolt-s27-003":["a.) *Nota notae est Nota rei ipsius* (a mark of the mark is a mark of the thing itself), this holds of affirmative judgments.",[]],"busolt-s27-004":["b.) *Repugnans notae repugnat rei ipsi* (what conflicts with the mark conflicts with the thing itself) this holds of negative inferences, e.g. To be alterable is a mark of everything divisible, to be divisible is a mark of a body, therefore by means of divisibility alterability is a mark of the body.",[]],"busolt-s27-005":["Every conclusion contains subject and predicate *Terminus medius* occurs in both premises; thus in the *Maiori* there will be predicate and *Terminus medius*, in the *minore* subject and *Terminus medius*. In such inferences of reason only the *Terminus medius* can be transposed. The way of transposition is fourfold, and from this arise precisely the 4. figures of syllogistics.",[]],"busolt-s27-006":["*Repugnans notae repugnat rei ipsi*. E.g. Nothing composite is simple, all matter is composite, therefore no matter is simple etc. To be simple holds of composites, and by virtue of the composite of bodies. The two rules cited are just this, which was for the ancients *dictum de omni et nullo*.",[]],"busolt-s27-007":["a.) *Dictum de omni*: whatever holds of the genus or species holds also of all individuals contained in the genus or species.",[]],"busolt-s27-008":["b.) *Dictum de nullo*: whatever conflicts with the genus or species conflicts also with all individuals contained in the genus or species.",[]],"busolt-s27-009":["The *Principium* of hypothetical and disjunctive inferences the author explains in the 364th and 365th §. There are thus only three *Principia* of inferences of reason for the *Consequentiae immediatae* are properly no inferences of reason; they are only consequences and need no rule. The author speaks of ordinary and extraordinary inferences of reason. But there is no other distinction in this, than that the inferences in which the form is hidden are extraordinary ones.",[]],"busolt-s28-001":["*Figura I.* Is better known than to need explanation, it is as it were the root of all conversion of the middle term in the others.",[]],"busolt-s28-002":["*Figura II.* Here the middle term is the predicate in the major and minor e.g. No thinking being is composite, all bodies are composite hence no body is a thinking being.",[]],"busolt-s28-003":["*Figura III.* Here the middle term in the major and minor proposition is the subject. E.g. All human beings are imperfect; some human beings are virtuous, therefore some virtuous ones are imperfect.",[]],"busolt-s28-004":["*Figura IV.* Here the middle term is predicate in the major and subject in the minor E.g. Whatever has a force of acting is spirit every spirit is substance, therefore every substance has a force of acting.",[]],"busolt-s29-001":["1.) In an ordinary inference of reason there can be no more than 3. chief concepts; for the conclusion has only 2 Remark If one of the two chief concepts in the premises is ambiguous; then the ambiguity is found in the whole inference E.g. In the place where I am, there is no other, the room that is the place where I am, therefore in the room there is no other.",[]],"busolt-s29-002":["2.) The *Medius Terminus* is not to enter the conclusion, that indeed concurs with the worst fault.",[]],"busolt-s29-003":["3.) *Ex puris negatiuis nihil sequitur* (from purely negative premises nothing follows). This one sees through sound reason; it follows from the nature of the thing itself: For the subsumption must be under the condition of the rule. I must say that that belongs to a thing which is the condition of the rule, and this must surely be affirmative. E.g. No human being is an irrational animal, No ox is a human being therefore no ox is an irrational animal.",[]],"busolt-s29-004":["4.) *Ex puris particularibus nihil sequitur* (from purely particular premises nothing follows). Because otherwise what stands under the condition of the rule cannot be applied to its predicate E.g. Some sentient beings are human beings, some irrational animals are sentient beings, therefore some irrational animals are human beings.",[]],"busolt-s29-005":["5.) *Conclusio sequi debet partem debiliorem* (the conclusion must follow the weaker part) NB: Among the premises one calls *partem debiliorem* that which is either particular or negative The proposition runs: if a premise is negative, then the conclusion is negative, if a premise is particular then the conclusion is particular. Ground: If I have subsumed only some things under the condition of the universal rule; then the predicate of the universal rule must also hold only of some if the rule is negative, then the negation of the rule must also hold of that which stands under the rule.",[]],"busolt-s29-006":["6.) In the conclusion neither more nor less is contained than in the premises, that is, let *notus* not be found more ample in the conclusion. There must thus not be inferred more than what is comprised in the premises in the *Praemissis* are *maior*, *minor* and *Terminus medius*. In the conclusion only *maior* and *minor*. The first figure is immediately evident. In the second and third figure one must first reflect what the sequence would be. The fourth figure the logicians have called the unnatural one, in this they probably noticed the entanglement. In the last figures the form can be very much hidden.",[]],"busolt-s29-007":["The rules of the first figure are:",[]],"busolt-s29-008":["a.) *Maior esse debet vniversalis* (the major must be universal).",[]],"busolt-s29-009":["b.) *Minor affirmans*.",[]],"busolt-s29-010":["These rules seem to limit the first figure but they rather show us its extension to all and every kind of inferences, the major must by virtue of the rule be universal, but it can also be affirmative or negative; the minor is to be *affirmans*, but it can also be universal and particular. Thus in the first figure inferences of all quality and quantity are possible, thus of A, E, I, *et* O In the others only some of them are ever possible; consequently the first figure is the genuine, the only natural and pure one. Remark The above rules hold for all and every inference of reason: for in every inference of reason there must always be a rule, but a rule is nothing other than a universal truth. Further, since in every inference of reason I am to subsume a concept, I must indeed assert that this concept belongs under a universal rule; hence affirm.",[]],"busolt-s29-011":["The rules of the second figure limit themselves merely to one kind of inferences, namely to negative conclusions!",[]],"busolt-s29-012":["The third figure can have only particular conclusions.",[]],"busolt-s29-013":["In the fourth figure only particular or negative conclusions are possible; the conclusion thus has only *partes debiliores*. From this it is evident that the first figure alone is the pure one, but that the others all have mixed inferences. *Ratiocinium purum* is where besides the major, minor and the conclusion no other proposition runs in.",[]],"busolt-s29-014":["*Ratiocinium impurum seu hybridum*, where to the 3 propositions a *consequentia immediata* must also be thought. It is thus a mating of a *ratiocinium* with a *consequentia immediata*, this takes place in the three last figures. The explanation from the second figure: no thinking being is composite all bodies are composite therefore no body is a thinking substance. – Here in weighing the major I think *per conversionem*: nothing composite is a thinking substance; I thus bring the second figure *per conuersionem maioris* into the first. The first conclusion must here be negative, for the second is to be brought *per conversionem* into the first, in this the major must be *vniversaliter*, so must, since only negative propositions can give a universal proposition *per conversionem simpliciter*, the conclusion also be negative: *Conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem*.",[]],"busolt-s29-015":["Explanation of the third figure: All human beings are imperfect some are virtuous; therefore some virtuous ones are imperfect. Here the *Terminus medius* does not stand in its fitting place, and this is given it merely by the reduction to the first figure. All human beings are imperfect some virtuous ones are human beings, therefore some virtuous ones are imperfect. In the reduction of the third figure, therefore, the *metathesis Terminorum* produces the minor, which indeed by *consequentiam immediatam*. From some virtuous ones are human beings, follows, some human beings are virtuous. The proof of the third figure, namely that merely particular conclusions take place in it, lies in this because here I must be able to reduce the minor to the first figure, but this minor must be *affirmans*, so it is necessary that, since always particular propositions can be converted *simpliciter* *per conseqventiam immediatam*, there also be a particular proposition in the *minori propositione* and then according to this as the *parti debiliori* be correct in the inference, hence the conclusion in the third figure must always be particular.",[]],"busolt-s29-016":["In the fourth figure the major is made into the minor, but from this it is now evident that the 3 last kinds of inference are unnatural, and merely confuse the understanding, hence must be banished from logic, which is to be a lighthouse of the soul; Every judgment has a *modus*, which expresses quantity and quality, such are signified by the vowels A, E, I, O. Of these four vowels only 3 can occur in a syllogism, because there are only 3 propositions.",[]],"busolt-s29-017":["From the transposition of these vowels arise 64 particular forms. But through the rule *ex puris negatiuis nihil sequitur* 16 drop out through *conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem* 24 drop out altogether only 19 are possible, among which some are corrupt so that only remain. To bring these to formulas, one has devised a certain technique of technical terms. One has added to the vowels also consonants, which however are also all arbitrary. The rules of the vowels are:",[]],"busolt-s29-018":["a.) *S vult simpliciter verti, P vero accidens. M vult transponi* (S wants to be converted simply, P however *per accidens*. M wants to be transposed), this happens *per Metathesin*, *per migrationem*.",[]],"busolt-s29-019":["b.) *C per impossibili duci*, *per contrapositionem*, the technical terms are called",[]],"busolt-s29-020":["In the first figure *Barbara, Celarent, Darii, Ferio*. In the second figure *Cesare, Camestres, Festino, Barocco*. In the third figure *Darapti, Felapton, Disamis, Datisi, Bocardo, Ferison*. In the fourth figure *Calemes, Bamalip, Dimatis, Fesapo, Fresisom*. All this that has been said up to now of syllogisms holds only of categorical inferences The hypothetical *Ratiocinia* are of a wholly different kind. The *Ratiocinia hypothetica* have 2 *Modos*:",[]],"busolt-s29-021":["a.) *Modum ponentem*. There I infer *a veritate antecedentis ad consequentis positionem* (from the truth of the antecedent to the positing of the consequent).",[]],"busolt-s29-022":["b.) *Modum tollentem*; here I infer *a remotione consequentis ad remotionem antecedentis* (from the removal of the consequent to the removal of the antecedent), e.g. if there is a blind chance, then there is no order in the world but the latter is false, therefore the first; that is *modus tollens*.",[]],"busolt-s29-023":["If bodies are divisible, then they are alterable; but the former is true, therefore the latter. The major of the proposition says nothing more than how one proposition follows from the other. If the *consequens* is incorrect; then one says it is inferred *a baculo ad angulum*. The *modus tollens* takes place only *a consequente ad antecedentem*, namely *sublato consequente tollitur antecedens*, but *a positione consequentis ad positionem antecedentis non valet modus ponens*. Disjunctive inferences of reason consist in this, that the major is divided into several parts, which are opposed to one another.",[]],"busolt-s29-024":["The *Membra diuidentia* we call in an inference of reason *membra disiunctiua*. In such an inference we infer *a negatigatione omnium membrorum disiunctionis praeter vnum ad positionem huius vnius*",[]],"busolt-s29-025":["With two *Membris* one infers *a negatione vnius ad positionem alterius*. If one is false; then the other is true. Or I infer also *a positione vnius membri disiunctionis ad negationem ceterorum omnium*.",[]],"busolt-s29-026":["A *dilemma* is that kind of arguing in which the major is hypothetical. All *dilemmata* have a negative conclusion e.g. If God had not created the best world, then he either would not have been able to create it, or not have wanted to create it. *Dicitur alius Syllogismus cornutus crocodilus.* That the ancients made much of the dilemma, when they drew other sequences from the assertions. A dilemma always has something sophistical about it, and is a *modus indirectus* of inferring.",[]],"busolt-s29-027":["A way of inferring is:",[]],"busolt-s29-028":["From the sequence to the ground, for the proposition is then not *affirmation*, because a sequence can have more than one ground. Only then can I infer according to this *modo* with truth, if I confirm the truth of all sequences as true To infer from the falsity of the sequences to the falsity of the ground is quite easy, for I need only show that one sequence is false. The dilemma has much that is convenient about it: for",[]],"busolt-s29-029":["a.) One does not prove straightaway, from the constitution of the thing",[]],"busolt-s29-030":["b.) Also negative propositions have more emphasis. They serve as it were for conviction. A contradiction always makes a greater relative impression, it is much more striking than a true proof, what holds of immediate sequences has just been said. Every *Ratiocinium* is either *Formale* or *crypticum*, a hidden inference of reason. We speak mostly through *Ratiocinia cryptica*. E.g. The soul, because it is simple, is imperishable. A *Ratiocinium formale* is that which is in accordance with the school. In the understanding every *Ratiocinium* is *formale*, in words not always. An *enthymema* is called an inference where one leaves out one of the premises, e.g. All human beings can err therefore I.",[]],"busolt-s29-031":["Inferences through *induction* and through *analogy*. Syllogisms are either apodictic or not. What I cognize through reason is apodictic. I cognize through it everything that is necessary; what I on the other hand cognize through experience is not apodictic The inferences of the understanding give merely an empirical certainty, but an empirical certainty can still be called necessity, namely if it helps as much as a necessity for the use of experience. The former are strict, the latter not strict inferences. In syllogisms we conclude from the universal the particular; in inferences of the understanding from the particular the universal. The former is *ratiocinium genuinum*, the latter *spurium*, which the author calls *ordinaire extraordinis*. But because from nothing but particular propositions nothing follows; the understanding is concerned through its perceptions here to make rules, i.e., universal propositions. Thus I see, e.g., merely through experience, that all human beings must die, because namely this one, that one and all human beings who have lived have died: I thus infer here conversely to in the case of the *ratiociniis*. What belongs to so many *individuals* as have ever been, that belongs to all others of this kind, i.e., what we call *induction*. This is the path that the understanding *continually* goes. Meanwhile there is here no apodictic certainty: for individual cases can still always make exceptions to our so-called rules, as, e.g., some human bodies are still found where the heart beats on the right side, and in general everything lies reversed. Notwithstanding this the *medicus* changes his *laudana*. It is just so with all languages and with the universal character of the nations, as one says, e.g.: that all people in France are polite, only the innkeepers not, and conversely in England all rude, only the innkeepers however polite on the whole here belongs the rule *nulla regula sine exceptione*. These exceptions one calls in disputing *instances* the rule has then only a reality, to infer in experience and there there can be an exception, if only the whole magnitude is not cancelled. Through *induction* we make universal propositions, and this inference is *a particulaire ad vniversale*. Otherwise there is also a kind of *inductions* from systematic propositions, i.e., when something is *predicated* of a genus, because it already lies in the *analysis* of the concept itself. E.g., the human being is rational. There is still a second way to infer *oblique*, namely through *analogy*. This kind of inference is distinguished from the previous one by this, if to two things as many pieces as I have perceived through the sharpest investigation are common, then they can also agree in all pieces which I cannot yet explore. I thus bring my inference not to several judgments but properties of these very same things. Thus we infer the *circulation* of the blood in animals from the otherwise similarity of the human and animal body in their *constitution*. Just so the inner structure of the polyps, in which we otherwise notice no entrails. These inferences through *induction* and *analogy* are as it were the two crutches of human *ratiocination* true crutches, for we go very unsurely Hence, it is a mere attempt. They say, e.g., all young either come into the world alive or are hatched from eggs. Notwithstanding this it was always known. that with polyps there are young in every cut-off part. *Sophisma* is a dialectical kind of inference, which merely has the illusion of truth. The illusion can lie",[]],"busolt-s29-032":["a.) In the matter, i.e., in *praemissis* or in the propositions themselves, or",[]],"busolt-s29-033":["b.) In the form, i.e., when the illusion lies in the *conclusion* (in the way of inferring).",[]],"busolt-s29-034":["Of the former we do not speak here, some *dialecticians* amused themselves with the second, others with the first. Such an inference one calls a *paralogical* one when, e.g., a *terminus* is ambiguous, e.g., No artist is born. The ancients had such an inference which they called the Liar: if you say you are lying and speak the truth, then you speak the truth, if you are lying. *Sophisma heterogenea, vbi Status quaestionis mutatur, dicitur etiam Sophisma ignorationis elenchi Hic pertinet illud alter mulget hircum, alter supponit cribrum.* *Copulative* sophisms are those where properly two *syllogisms* are combined as: Everything simple is immortal, God and all spirits are simple.",[]],"busolt-s29-035":["The *ars Polysyllogistica* is when we chain many inferences with one another, and in it the *conclusion* of the previous inference becomes the *premise* of the following one, and we infer here always in *episyllogisms*, and thus the polysyllogistic is *progrediens*; but if I go back to the *deduction* and prove one of the *premises* then the polysyllogistic is *regrediens*. This seems otherwise to be advantageously applied. Especially in disputing; but great caution is necessary in it, for if in the *polysyllogistic progrediens* I grant the first *premise*, and do not properly investigate its correctness in matter and form then true things can always be concluded, which however stand as well as fall with the *premise*. So that therefore no error turn out in disputing herein; it is advisable that the *conclusion* of the *opponent* always give the assertion of the *respondent*. The mentioned error in the progredient *syllogisms* has not seldom crept into philosophical systems. The regredient *syllogistic* on the other hand has far more advantages over the first: For I criticize here whether the ground is true and the sequel correct. *Sorites* is merely a consequence of the expression. There I heap *conclusion* on *conclusion* and leave out the *premises*. Such a chaining of inferences can now consist of categorical, hypothetical propositions and so there becomes *Sorites kategorikus Sorites Hypotheticus*. *Circulus in demonstrando, saltus in probando* happens when I infer *a ratione remota* at once *ad rationatum*. Here therefore the sequel is not clear, and I leave out a necessary intermediate inference.",[]],"busolt-s29-036":["*Petitio Principii*. *Petere Principium* means to beg a *principium*, *postulare principium* is to demand that one may prove that to him. A proof which demonstrates too little is therefore to be regarded not as false but as deficient, for I prove something, and something can still always be added. But a proof which proves too much is false and proves nothing at all.",[]],"busolt-s30-001":["The *Topica* of Aristotle were nothing other than a *recension* of all titles and *argumentae* under which objects can be considered; as we, e.g., can consider a matter from different points of view. *Locus topicus* is a title alleged in logic, under which an object can be considered and treated. I can consider a thing *in genere* and *in differentia specifica*, according to distinctness and indistinctness; as ground and sequel, as *axiom* and theorem etc. Thus there is a metaphysical place *Locus metaphysicus loci rerum sunt etiam fines rerum*. There are *locos grammaticos* there I can consider an object according to its *definition*. These *Locos Logicos*, because they concern only the form, one calls common kinds, *Locos communes* or better common propositions. *Locorum communium vsus et in logicis et in rhetoricis locum habet.* *Loci ethici* treats of *decore* and *honesto* *Loci physici*, whether I derive a thing from *causis efficientibus* or *finalibus*.",[]],"busolt-s31-001":["Just as the theoretical part of logic presents the universal condition of the possible perfection of logical cognition, so practical logic is a universal science which contains the condition for attaining to a possible logically perfect cognition. Here therefore the question is: how shall I now attain to possible logical perfection in cognition, where apply this, where that? Here one should beware of tautological definitions: For mostly one is accustomed to dispatch the solution thus; one should do what is required for it. One should rather say instead: Acquaintance with the *object* at hand gives here the best solution. For herein it depends on the matter alone, and this even practical logic itself cannot have as its object. Only so much as belongs to the form can it teach us to apply all rules of the theory; thus only the application The chief object of practical logic is accordingly the method, i.e., the ordering of manifold cognition with all logical perfection, insofar as it is to be taken together into a whole. Practical logic could accordingly also be called the pure universal doctrine of method. To method is *contradistinguished* manner. This latter requires merely common way of teaching, and everyone therefore has his manner, his taste, yet *popularity* is the chief piece in it. Manner is a way of treating the *object* according to laws of reason. Manner can accordingly, like everything empirical, not be brought into rules. Yet *popularity* is the most praiseworthy property in it: for it presupposes logical perfection, since it is nothing other than *accomodatio Logicae cognitionis ad sensum communem*. One shows here without any art how our concepts gradually expand without scholastic strictness. Further, manner and method are distinguished by this, that method belongs to science, manner merely to entertainment; just as this very one makes itself known through inclination and practice. In the latter even the errors are already: for it is nature; just as with womenfolk even the spots in the face make the beauty more prominent.",[]],"busolt-s31-002":["To method is opposed:",[]],"busolt-s31-003":["a.) The *tumultuary*, where one observes no order in thinking; yet in *conceiving* the tumultuary is permitted, and advantageous;",[]],"busolt-s31-004":["for one looks about as it were quite freely in the field of objects; whereas method sets us limitations in consideration: for the tumultuary serves for manifoldness, method for unity. This tumultuary in method is always a proof of a tumultuary way of thinking.",[]],"busolt-s31-005":["b.) There is a kind of presentations which is *rhapsodistic*, by virtue of which one can always have observed an order in thinking, only one is not able to make the guiding thread of one's consideration visible. This reveals itself especially in the lack of titles of the sections; in short what is due and right of method is lacking; for that makes each other clear.",[]],"busolt-s31-006":["c.) *Aphoristic* one calls a presentation when method indeed shines forth in thinking and even in the presentation, only the transition from one part to another is not distinctly represented, when there is unity in thinking but not *continuity*. As it were the preceding chapter contains no *propaedeutic* for the transition to the following, the aphorists surprise us as it were, and become not seldom agreeable. Whereas a book of rhapsodistic method is unbearable, since one cannot at once so soon look up in the archive of memory. in the aphoristic method it is as it were as if I jump from one stone to another which lie in a straight line.",[]],"busolt-s31-007":["d.) Finally the *popular* method, as the most generally useful, deserves to be preferred to all others. For it is as it were the condescension of a man of science to the common human understanding.",[]],"busolt-s31-008":["Method proper has all too much exertion of the understanding, and then it forbids every kind of reconciliation In *popularity* otherwise the French have the great advantage, and a Fontenelle can make a mathematical proposition just as comprehensible as an aesthetic truth. Method is also divided into the analytic and synthetic. In the synthetic one goes from principles to *principata*, in the other conversely, thus the whole of mathematics is synthetic. The analytic is as it were an *inverse synthesis*, and is philosophy. Further, method is divided:",[]],"busolt-s31-009":["a.) Into the *scientific* this belongs for scholars and one looks in it especially to thoroughness. it presents everything *in abstracto*, namely order and distinctness is the chief work in it.",[]],"busolt-s31-010":["b.) Into the popular this looks also to the ideas, since in it everything is presented *in concreto*. Thus Hume writes reflections on the human understanding in *scientific* method, but with little approval. This very matter he treated in popular method, brought in comparisons from rhetoric, poetry, sensation etc. etc., and the book was universally approved. Remark on the previous. The synthetic method is appropriate only to the sciences where one goes from the universal to the particular, thus to mathematics; whereas in philosophy the analytic most takes place in some parts excepted. The mathematical method is the method proper. This very one applied to other sciences is merely in vain otherwise one falls into the vice of method-manner. There is still the division of method into the:",[]],"busolt-s31-011":["a.) Natural, namely *objectively* natural, which is appropriate to the object. Method *in concreto* is a natural one",[]],"busolt-s31-012":["b.) The artificial. If this artificial one is to have no error, it must be nature; for art is actually nothing other than cultivated nature. This very thing one calls *naivety*, namely an art which seems nature, or a nature which seems art. Further the syllogistic way of teaching, such there is not properly, and must also not be tolerated: for whom do the formal inferences not vex. Indeed with individual propositions, in disputing it is permitted. The *tabular* method is that where one sketches the skeleton of a system. The *dialogical* method instructs through *discoursing*. It is twofold:",[]],"busolt-s31-013":["a.) *Socratic*, where the teacher poses as pupil, and through analysis seeks to decide the question from the pupil himself. It is called so because Socrates especially made use of it in his dialogues.",[]],"busolt-s31-014":["b.) *Catechetical*, this is merely for cognitions of memory, for there the teacher gives them to the pupil himself. The *geographical* method is that which presents the objects which are simultaneous.",[]],"busolt-s31-015":["The *chronological* method is that which presents the objects which follow one another in time. There is still the division into *doctrine* and *discipline*. *Doctrine* expands cognitions and is thus positive. *Discipline* merely keeps off error, is thus negative. *Tumultuary* meditation has much that is advantageous in it: for through this kind of raiding, where we from the footpath, without stopping everywhere",[]],"busolt-s31-016":["plunder from every region, our cognition certainly gains not a little.",[]],"busolt-s32-001":["In *meditating* one needs an *object*, consequently one must collect the materials and this goes best through tumultuary thinking. For the further promotion of this one *peruses* books, especially when it concerns historical or polemical matters: otherwise however the thinking of others gives us ourselves occasion for new ideas and for new thoughts.",[]],"busolt-s32-002":["Lambert used *lexica*, and sought out *synonyma*, *analogies* etc. etc., and thus one attains manifoldness. This one observes in thinking; in writing down however unity is required, the side thoughts occurring to us as it were on the way one notes in the margin, and afterwards divides them into their compartments. But because our *imagination*, as soon as it has once been brought on a journey, does not so soon leave off from it; one must, so that one not always consider the object only from one point of view, do oneself violence to break off often, to read books of quite different matter, and as it were to make a *diversion*. In the morning one must bring the materials for a system into order in the evening on the other hand collect materials: for the *imagination* roams about as it were in the evening occupied with many objects in the morning however it is as it were under the supervision of reason.",[]],"busolt-s33-001":["Speaking and talking are distinguished. Speaking happens in order to make oneself understood; talking in order to attain one's end. How now shall the philosopher arrange his language? In general the philosopher looks more to the meaning of words than to the style. The *principium* of this is: The expression must be appropriate to the concept. He can therefore also use the language of scholars, that is not only dead language but also technical *termini*. Yet these are only so to speak a makeshift, and he does not depart without deliberation and need from the common use of language; but for the philosophical principles in respect of expression a language is seldom sufficient. German seems, next to Greek, to be most philosophical; for it is the very most correct, it has the most *synonyma*. Notwithstanding this one still seldom finds two words in the whole language which would be one and the same: For the multitude of synonyms is always a proof of wealth of the language. For one can by means of synonyms determine the different degrees of exactness in the *object*. The cause of the wealth of the German language one derives fittingly from its missionaries, who, when they wanted to convert them formed many of their already invented words after the Greek and Latin as from *compassio* Mitleiden, *misericordia* Barmherzigkeit. Precisely because these missionaries, all improvers of the German language, wanted to enlighten the understanding of the Germans, they also had to speak little *tropically*, and therefore they had out of need to enrich the Welsh language with quite newer words, since otherwise these very *tropi* are also a proof of the poverty of a language. Precisely when a language has many *tropos*; this gives occasion to various plays on words and witty notions.",[]],"busolt-s33-002":["Word-coining is a great error. Of course one says *verba valent sicut nummi*, but it is still only the high sovereign authority of the land that has the right of coinage otherwise however it remains true",[]],"busolt-s33-003":["*Penes vsum est et ius et norma loquendi*",[]],"busolt-s33-004":["*Finis.*",[]]};
